{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03527","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03527","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03527","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sayan","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Sayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shahzaib Saqib","familyName":"Warraich","name":"Warraich, Shahzaib Saqib","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dhruv","familyName":"Tarsadiya","name":"Tarsadiya, Dhruv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gregory","familyName":"Yauney","name":"Yauney, Gregory","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Swabha","familyName":"Swayamdipta","name":"Swayamdipta, Swabha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sample, Align, Synthesize: Graph-Based Response Synthesis with ConGrs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T21:50:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:12:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-15T22:47:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-19T00:21:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Language models can be sampled multiple times to access the distribution underlying their responses, but existing methods cannot efficiently synthesize rich epistemic signals across different long-form responses. We introduce Consensus Graphs (ConGrs), a flexible DAG-based data structure that represents shared information, as well as semantic variation in a set of sampled LM responses to the same prompt. We construct ConGrs using a light-weight lexical sequence alignment algorithm from bioinformatics, supplemented by the targeted usage of a secondary LM judge. Further, we design task-dependent decoding methods to synthesize a single, final response from our ConGr data structure. Our experiments show that synthesizing responses from ConGrs improves factual precision on two biography generation tasks by up to 31% over an average response and reduces reliance on LM judges by more than 80% compared to other methods. We also use ConGrs for three refusal-based tasks requiring abstention on unanswerable queries and find that abstention rate is increased by up to 56%. We apply our approach to the MATH and AIME reasoning tasks and find an improvement over self-verification and majority vote baselines by up to 6 points of accuracy. We show that ConGrs provide a flexible method for capturing variation in LM responses and using the epistemic signals provided by response variation to synthesize more effective responses."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published at COLM 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03527","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:52:06Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:52:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-19T02:53:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03245","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03245","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03245","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Yavari","name":"Yavari, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alireza","familyName":"Mohamadi","name":"Mohamadi, Alireza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elham","familyName":"Beydaghi","name":"Beydaghi, Elham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philipp","familyName":"Seeböck","name":"Seeböck, Philipp","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rainer A.","familyName":"Leitgeb","name":"Leitgeb, Rainer A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Frequency-Aware Model Parameter Explorer: A new attribution method for improving explainability"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-25T15:00:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:00:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-08T15:49:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-11T01:10:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-12T17:14:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-14T02:47:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"State-of-the-art attribution methods such as AttEXplore rely on adversarial sample generation that applies an all-pass filter across the frequency spectrum: they perturb all frequencies of the input image alike, from the low frequencies that carry coarse shape to the high frequencies that carry edges and textures. However, models rely on low and high frequencies to different degrees, and such an all-pass perturbation cannot adapt to the model being explained, which limits the faithfulness of the resulting attribution maps. We propose FAMPE (Frequency-Aware Model Parameter Explorer), an attribution method that overcomes this limitation by perturbing the low- and high-frequency bands of the input independently, so that the perturbation can be shifted toward the band that matters for the model. The boundary between the two bands is set for each image automatically from its spectral energy. On ImageNet with five convolutional neural network and Vision Transformer architectures, FAMPE significantly improves the Insertion Score over the strongest baseline, AttEXplore, on four of them, by up to 15.4%, at essentially the same computational cost. How the perturbed frequency bands are weighted matters: the best and the worst balance between them differ by 19-36% in Insertion Score, and the best balance depends on the architecture. Choosing the balance for each image with a probe that needs no ground truth raises the Insertion Score significantly further on all five architectures, reaching up to 24.4% above AttEXplore. Controlling which frequency band is perturbed is therefore a simple, low-cost way to improve attribution faithfulness."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03245","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:12Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:20:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04532","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04532","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04532","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xurui","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Xurui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Huai","name":"Huai, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"JingJing","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, JingJing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"More Than Meets the Eye? Uncovering the Reasoning-Planning Disconnect in Training Vision-Language Driving Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T06:50:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:17:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-08T02:08:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-09T00:26:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Model (VLM) driving agents promise explainable end-to-end autonomy by first producing natural-language reasoning and then predicting trajectory planning. However, whether planning is causally driven by this reasoning remains a critical but unverified assumption. To investigate this, we build DriveMind, a large-scale driving Visual Question Answering corpus with plan-aligned Chain-of-Thought (CoT), automatically generated from nuPlan. Our data generation process converts sensors and annotations into structured inputs and, crucially, separates priors from to-be-reasoned signals, enabling clean information ablations. Using DriveMind, we train representative VLM agents with Supervised Fine-Tuning and Group Relative Policy Optimization and evaluate them with nuPlan's metrics. Our results, unfortunately, indicate a consistent causal disconnect in reasoning-planning: removing ego/navigation priors causes large drops in planning scores, whereas removing CoT produces only minor changes. Attention analysis further shows that planning primarily focuses on priors rather than the CoT. Based on this evidence, we propose the Reasoning-Planning Decoupling Hypothesis, positing that the training-yielded reasoning is an ancillary byproduct rather than a causal mediator. To enable efficient diagnosis, we introduce a novel, training-free probe that measures an agent's reliance on priors by evaluating its planning robustness against minor input perturbations. In summary, we provide the community with a new dataset and a diagnostic tool to evaluate the causal fidelity of future models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04532","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:16:48Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:16:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-09T03:15:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04302","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04302","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04302","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas F","familyName":"Burns","name":"Burns, Thomas F","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Guessing is Rewarded: Rethinking Language Model Evaluation with Distributional Uncertainty Scoring"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T17:50:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:03:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-01T15:50:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-02T01:24:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Standard language model evaluation assigns scores to single predicted answers, rewarding high-confidence responses regardless of how residual probability mass is distributed over alternative options. This creates a systematic pressure toward overconfident guessing: under accuracy-based schemes, a model maximises its expected score by always committing to an answer rather than abstaining, even when its uncertainty is high. While penalty-based approaches partially address this by raising the confidence threshold for strategic guessing, they still treat all sub-threshold responses identically, ignoring a fundamental distinction in how models can express uncertainty - for example between hedging toward incorrect answers versus hedging toward \"I don't know\" responses. This paper introduces a novel evaluation metric to solve this problem of not considering a model's entire probability distribution over answer choices. The metric naturally distinguishes between harmful overconfidence in wrong answers and uncertainty expressed through abstention, providing scores in an interpretable default range. Through theoretical analysis and illustrative examples, the metric is shown to offer a more nuanced and aligned evaluation paradigm that incentivises models to express genuine uncertainty rather than guessing. Adapting 12 existing evaluation benchmarks to the metric's variants and measuring performance on six language models shows that for half of the tested benchmarks scores are negative across all tested models, indicating significant tendencies towards hallucination."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"32 pages, 2 figures; accepted to NeurIPS 2026 (Evaluations and Datasets track)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04302","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:11:11Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:11:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-02T03:28:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04525","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04525","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04525","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satoshi","familyName":"Hayakawa","name":"Hayakawa, Satoshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhta","familyName":"Takida","name":"Takida, Yuhta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masaaki","familyName":"Imaizumi","name":"Imaizumi, Masaaki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiromi","familyName":"Wakaki","name":"Wakaki, Hiromi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuki","familyName":"Mitsufuji","name":"Mitsufuji, Yuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Demystifying MaskGIT Sampler and Beyond: Adaptive Order Selection in Masked Diffusion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T06:30:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:16:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-31T05:31:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-23T00:52:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-28T07:09:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-29T02:20:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Masked diffusion models have shown promising performance in generating high-quality samples in a wide range of domains, but accelerating their sampling process remains relatively underexplored. To investigate efficient samplers for masked diffusion, this paper theoretically analyzes the MaskGIT sampler for image modeling, revealing its implicit temperature sampling mechanism. Through this analysis, we show that MaskGIT is asymptotically equivalent to a choose-then-sample (CTS) formulation, instantiated as the \"moment sampler,\" which explicitly separates index selection from token sampling. This CTS reformulation is essential: it yields unbiased token sampling and exposes an algorithmic design space for index selection, both of which are inaccessible in MaskGIT's original formulation. Regarding token sampling, we reveal that MaskGIT implicitly adopts a low-temperature sampler, which explains why MaskGIT often degrades with more sampling steps. The CTS reformulation of MaskGIT allows us to fix the temperature sampling to ensure unbiasedness. We also improve the index selection in CTS through two key innovations: a partial caching technique for transformers that approximates longer sampling trajectories without proportional computational cost, and a hybrid approach formalizing the exploration-exploitation trade-off in adaptive unmasking. Experiments in image and text domains demonstrate our theory as well as the efficiency of our proposed methods, advancing both theoretical understanding and practical implementation of masked diffusion samplers."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"TMLR Featured Certification"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04525","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:16:38Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:16:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-29T06:56:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03240","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03240","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03240","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongbo","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Hongbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Evans","name":"Evans, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generalization and the Rise of System-level Creativity in Science"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Digital Libraries (cs.DL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T18:56:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:00:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-17T23:13:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-21T00:14:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-10T16:56:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-14T00:05:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-14T13:00:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-15T00:51:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-08T21:51:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:47:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-09-20T03:20:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-09-22T00:57:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Scientific progress is widely understood as combinatorial, yet existing measures focus on the inputs a paper draws together, saying little about how knowledge is supplied for downstream reuse. Analyzing citation networks from tens of millions of publications in OpenAlex and the Web of Science, we decompose scientific contributions into three functional types, foundations, extensions, and generalizations, distinguishable by the roles they play in future science. Foundational and extensional works, which build and elaborate ideas within disciplines, dominated the post-war decades but declined steadily after the 1990s, while generalizations that catalyze innovation across many contexts rose sharply. Challenging the narrative of declining innovation inferred from the disruption index, our findings suggest the locus of innovation has shifted from within disciplines to between them, a transition the Internet and large language models may have facilitated. Our indices disentangle innovation modes conflated by existing measures, enabling reinterpretation of landmark findings of 'disruptive' science."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"134 pages, 79 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03240","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":5,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:04Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-22T04:43:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03348","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03348","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03348","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vladimir","familyName":"Yugay","name":"Yugay, Vladimir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duy-Kien","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Duy-Kien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Theo","familyName":"Gevers","name":"Gevers, Theo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cees G. M.","familyName":"Snoek","name":"Snoek, Cees G. M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin R.","familyName":"Oswald","name":"Oswald, Martin R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Monocular Visual Odometry without Calibration or Test-time Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:00:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:03:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-19T08:55:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-20T01:35:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-09T13:18:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-10T02:10:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-05T09:15:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-09T00:29:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-17T17:48:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-09-18T01:16:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The most accurate monocular visual odometry systems require known camera intrinsics, refine their estimates with test-time optimization, and recover trajectories only up to an unknown factor. Systems built on large 3D models need no intrinsics, but they remain considerably less accurate and slower for odometry. Direct pose regression avoids all these requirements, yet it has not matched either approach's accuracy. We revisit this formulation with a transformer that predicts relative camera poses together with separate rotation and translation confidences over overlapping image windows, supervised by camera poses alone. A confidence-weighted module then aggregates the overlapping predictions into a single trajectory. The resulting method, CalfVO, needs no intrinsics, no bundle adjustment, and no loop closure, and it recovers scale from learned priors, accurately enough that it is evaluated without any alignment to the ground truth. Across five benchmarks, it is the most accurate calibration-free method on every metric we report, and it runs at 53 FPS, faster than every baseline."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03348","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:47:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:47:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-18T02:44:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04593","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04593","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04593","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhao","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Wenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhikang","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Zhikang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyue","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Ziyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaidi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Kaidi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peijie","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Peijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingyang","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Qingyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xie","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"UniVoice: Unifying Autoregressive ASR and Flow-Matching based TTS with Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:47:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:20:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-20T02:49:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-21T01:14:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-08T05:49:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-09T01:23:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-04T08:15:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-07T00:33:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated promising performance in both automatic speech recognition (ASR) and text-to-speech (TTS) systems, gradually becoming the mainstream approach. However, most current approaches address these tasks separately rather than through a unified framework. This work aims to integrate these two tasks into one unified model. Although discrete speech tokenization enables joint modeling, its inherent information loss limits performance in both recognition and generation. In this work, we present UniVoice, a unified LLM framework through continuous representations that seamlessly integrates speech recognition and synthesis within a single model. Our approach combines the strengths of autoregressive modeling for speech recognition with flow matching for high-quality generation. To mitigate the inherent divergence between autoregressive and flow-matching models, we further design a dual attention mechanism, which switches between a causal mask for recognition and a bidirectional attention mask for synthesis. Furthermore, the proposed text-prefix-conditioned speech infilling method enables high-fidelity zero-shot voice cloning. Experimental results demonstrate that our method can achieve or exceed current single-task modeling methods in both ASR and zero-shot TTS tasks. This work explores new possibilities for end-to-end speech understanding and generation. Code is available at https://github.com/gwh22/UniVoice."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"accepted at interspeech2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04593","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:18:17Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:18:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-07T02:15:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03784","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03784","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03784","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruoxi","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Ruoxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotian","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Haotian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingpu","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Jingpu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Penghao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Penghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianxiao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qianxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Performance-Efficiency Tradeoffs in Transformers: An Approximation Theory Perspective"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T11:22:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:32:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-01T14:01:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-02T01:05:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformers have achieved remarkable successes across a wide range of applications, yet the theoretical foundation of their model efficiency remains underexplored. In this work, we investigate how the model parameters -- mainly attention heads and head dimensions -- should be allocated across layers to balance expressivity and efficiency. We first provide mathematical analysis on the role of early layers in information extraction from an approximation perspective, with a theoretical characterization on the trade-off between the number of heads and head dimension under a fixed parameter budget. In addition, we uncover and prove the \\emph{saturation} behavior of softmax activations: Continuously increasing head dimensions can lead to diminishing returns in learning errors, particularly for long sequences. Supported by both theory and experiments, this saturation pattern suggests that later layers can operate more efficiently with reduced parameters. Combining these insights, we propose principled strategies for allocating attention heads and dimensions across Transformers' layers, shedding light on theoretically-grounded model efficiency of Transformer-based architectures."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03784","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:58:22Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:58:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-02T02:59:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04226","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04226","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04226","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dustin","familyName":"Wright","name":"Wright, Dustin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarah","familyName":"Masud","name":"Masud, Sarah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jared","familyName":"Moore","name":"Moore, Jared","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Srishti","familyName":"Yadav","name":"Yadav, Srishti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maria","familyName":"Antoniak","name":"Antoniak, Maria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter Ebert","familyName":"Christensen","name":"Christensen, Peter Ebert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chan Young","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Chan Young","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Isabelle","familyName":"Augenstein","name":"Augenstein, Isabelle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"What and Whose Knowledge? Measuring Epistemic Diversity in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T14:29:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:58:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T16:07:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:57:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T07:35:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:30:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-30T14:52:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10-31T00:51:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-11-11T18:13:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-11-12T02:03:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-01-28T13:27:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-01-29T01:45:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-08-31T12:37:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v7"},{"date":"2026-09-01T02:02:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v7"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"7","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used as primary knowledge sources, yet their epistemic diversity - defined as the diversity of real-world claims in their outputs - has never been measured. Low epistemic diversity would pose a risk of knowledge collapse as homogeneous LLMs mediate a shrinking in the range of accessible information over time. The dominant paradigm is that overall LLM diversity is low, but this is always with respect to a single point in time, with no reference baseline or consideration for variation across countries. We address this gap in knowledge by performing the first systematic study of epistemic diversity in LLMs across time and cultural context, testing 27 LLMs on 155 topics covering 12 countries, resulting in 1.7M responses and 70M individual claims. We find that epistemic diversity has increased substantially over the past three years, a positive counter to recent diversity pessimism. However, despite progress, we find that every system is less diverse than a search baseline. This gap is not uniform: RAG can improve diversity, while large models are counterintuitively less diverse than smaller ones. Moreover, LLM parametric knowledge systematically reflects English over local-language knowledge for country specific topics. Together, these results demonstrate that while progress on epistemic diversity is tangible, it is insufficient and unevenly distributed."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2026; 18 pages, 8 figures, 4 tables; v2: Fixed the modeling for table 3, random effect is the model version; v3: Fixed minor formatting issues in tables 2 and 3; v4: Fixed some typos and model description; v5: Updated metadata; v6: Improved search baseline, writing revisions, added comparisons to semantic similarity only approaches; v7 changelog: EMNLP camera ready"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04226","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":6,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:09:20Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:09:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:01:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04617","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04617","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04617","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhejian","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Zhejian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Geng","name":"Geng, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhijun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiahuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongxiang","familyName":"Weng","name":"Weng, Rongxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jingang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuezhi","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Xuezhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xunliang","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Xunliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shujian","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Shujian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AdaR: A Framework for Equipping LLMs with Adaptive Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:30:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:22:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T03:08:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:26:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-24T07:59:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-25T01:23:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mathematical reasoning is a primary indicator of large language models (LLMs) intelligence. However, existing LLMs exhibit failures in robustness and generalization. This paper attributes these deficiencies to spurious reasoning, wherein generated reasoning traces are driven by superficial correlations, leading models to blindly reproduce memorized patterns from the training data. To address this challenge, we propose the AdaR framework to equip LLMs with adaptive reasoning, wherein models establish correct correlations between query templates and reasoning processes. AdaR automatically synthesizes logically equivalent queries by varying variable values and trains models using Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) to penalize spurious logic while encouraging adaptive logic. To ensure data quality, we extract the problem-solving logic from the original query, generate the corresponding answer through code execution, and then apply a sanity check. Experimental results demonstrate that AdaR achieves substantial improvements in mathematical reasoning while maintaining high data efficiency. Furthermore, even advanced LLMs still exhibit deficiencies in robustness and generalization, which our work effectively mitigates. Our project is available at https://github.com/NJUNLP/AdaR."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04617","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:18:51Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:18:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T03:50:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03536","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03536","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03536","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaohan","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Zhaohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zaiqiao","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Zaiqiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siwei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Siwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iadh","familyName":"Ounis","name":"Ounis, Iadh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GraphMed-LT: Patient-Specific Graph Memory with Latent Clinical Thought Refinement for Multi-Turn Medical Conversations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T22:11:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:12:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-14T11:40:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T00:44:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-22T23:26:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-25T00:43:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-turn medical question answering (QA) aims to model realistic clinical diagnosis, where a doctor gathers patient information across multiple turns of conversation. Existing multi-turn medical conversation systems have shown promising progress, but they often rely on accumulated conversation histories as memory, leaving clinical evidence fragmented across turns. We propose GraphMed-LT, a patient-specific graph memory approach with latent clinical thought refinement for multi-turn medical conversations. GraphMed-LT extracts patient-specific clinical triplets from patient responses, retrieves relevant knowledge triplets, and organises them into an incrementally updated graph memory. The graph memory is projected into graph-conditioned evidence tokens and refined inside a trainable doctor agent through hidden-state feedback, enabling the agent to update its internal clinical context before asking follow-up questions or producing the final answer. Experiments on three multi-turn medical QA benchmarks show that GraphMed-LT consistently outperforms existing multi-turn medical conversation baselines across multiple LLM backbones, achieving up to a 6.3 percentage-point absolute improvement over the strongest baseline. Further analyses show that GraphMed-LT asks more answerable follow-up questions and provides consistent gains across medical specialties."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03536","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:52:18Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:52:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T03:50:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03519","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03519","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03519","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangxu","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Fangxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Hongyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TS-Reasoner: Aligning Time Series Foundation Models with LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T21:20:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:11:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-01T20:02:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:44:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-19T18:00:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-21T00:03:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Time series reasoning is crucial to decision-making in diverse domains, including finance, energy, and scientific discovery. While existing time series foundation models (TSFMs) can capture low-level dynamic patterns and provide accurate forecasting, further analysis usually requires additional background knowledge and sophisticated reasoning, which are lacking in most TSFMs but can be achieved through Large Language Models (LLMs). On the other hand, without expensive post-training, LLMs often struggle with the numerical understanding of time series data. Although it is intuitive to integrate the two types of models, developing effective training recipes that align the two modalities for reasoning tasks is still an open challenge. To this end, we propose TS-Reasoner that aligns the latent representations of TSFMs with the textual inputs of LLMs for downstream understanding/reasoning tasks. Specifically, we propose a simple yet effective method to curate diverse, synthetic pairs of time series and textual captions for alignment training. We then develop a two-stage training recipe that applies instruction fine-tuning after the alignment pretraining. Unlike existing works that train an LLM to take time series as inputs, we leverage a pretrained TSFM and freeze it during training. Experiments on several benchmarks demonstrate that TS-Reasoner not only outperforms a wide range of open-source LLMs, Vision-Language Models (VLMs), and Time Series LLMs of comparable scale, but also does so with remarkable data efficiency, e.g., using less than half the training data."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to Transactions on Machine Learning Research, 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03519","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:51:54Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:51:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-21T02:22:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03880","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03880","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03880","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunhao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yunhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sijing","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Sijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Kang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yucheng","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yucheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangtao","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Guangtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploring Instruction Data Quality for Explainable Image Quality Assessment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T17:12:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:39:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-13T16:47:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-14T01:02:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In recent years, with the rapid development of large multimodal models (LMMs), explainable image quality assessment (IQA) has attracted increasing attention, aiming to understand the perceptual quality problems of images. Existing studies typically construct large-scale instruction tuning datasets to enhance the quality perception capabilities of LMMs, following the data scaling law. However, as the fundamental capabilities of LMMs continue to improve, existing instruction tuning datasets may contain redundant and less challenging samples, resulting in substantial computational costs. In this paper, we investigate whether data quantity remains the dominant factor in LMM instruction tuning for explainable IQA. Based on a strong pre-trained LMM, we observe that randomly selecting a properly sized subset of training data can outperform full-data fine-tuning, indicating substantial redundancy in existing instruction tuning datasets. Motivated by this observation, we propose Q-Selector, a clustering-based data selection framework consisting of three stages: hierarchical LMM-based clustering feature extraction, cluster quota allocation through density and transferability, and SVD-based cluster sampling strategy. Specifically, Q-Selector extracts multi-layer features from the vision encoder, text encoder, and large language model components of LMMs as clustering features. It then determines the sampling quota for each cluster based on inter-cluster transferability and intra-cluster density. Finally, Q-Selector employs a Singular Value Decomposition (SVD)-based sampling strategy to select high-quality instruction data. Experimental results demonstrate that Q-Selector achieves 102.1% and 103.7% of the performance of full-data fine-tuning using only 10% of the training data on explainable IQA and image aesthetics assessment tasks, respectively."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03880","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:00:46Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:00:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-14T02:22:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04465","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04465","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04465","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiping","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi Evie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yi Evie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Freda","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Freda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tianshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Autonomy Reshapes How Personalization Affects Privacy Concerns and Trust in LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T03:38:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:13:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-06T00:31:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-07T01:07:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-09T12:40:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-11T01:04:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM agents require personal information for personalization in order to effectively act on users' behalf, but this raises privacy concerns that can discourage data sharing, limiting both the autonomy levels at which agents can operate and the effectiveness of personalization. Yet the expanded design space of agent autonomy also presents opportunities to shape these effects, which remain underexplored. We conducted a $3\\times3$ between-subjects experiment ($N=450$) to study how agent autonomy level influences personalization's effects on users' privacy concerns, trust, and willingness to use, as well as the underlying psychological processes. We find that risk-contingent autonomy, where the agent delegates control back to users upon detecting potential privacy leakage, improves users' perceived control. This in turn attenuates personalization's adverse effects: privacy concerns rise less and trust declines less. Our results suggest that designing $\\textbf{agent's autonomy}$ that supports $\\textbf{human autonomy}$ (both perceived control and oversight effectiveness) helps users benefit from personalization without being deterred by growing privacy concerns. This positions aligning decision authority between users and agents ($\\textbf{autonomy alignment}$) as an important opportunity and challenge for trustworthy human-agent interaction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to COLM 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04465","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:15:11Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:15:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:12:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03997","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03997","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03997","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjie","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Junjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arshana","familyName":"Welivita","name":"Welivita, Arshana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeleikun","familyName":"Di","name":"Di, Zeleikun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingfu","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jingfu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuzhe","familyName":"Zhi","name":"Zhi, Xuzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ritu","familyName":"Agarwal","name":"Agarwal, Ritu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gordon","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Gordon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mapping Patient-Perceived Physician Traits from Nationwide Online Reviews with LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T02:16:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:46:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-06T15:01:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-07T00:53:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding how patients perceive their physicians is essential to improving trust, communication, and satisfaction. Patients increasingly consult large language models (LLMs) to summarize physician reviews and shape provider choices, yet the national landscape of patient-perceived physician traits remains poorly characterized. We present an LLM-based pipeline that extracts ten patient-perceived physician trait scores from review text: five Big-Five-style and five patient-oriented dimensions. From one million U.S. physicians, we analyze 4.1 million reviews of 226,999 physicians. We validate the pipeline through multi-model comparison and human expert benchmarking. LLM and human-rater trait scores from reviews are consistent. Trait scores correlate strongly with review rating scores yet retain substantial independent variance. Two national-scale patterns emerge: male physicians receive higher trait scores across all traits, with the largest gap in clinical competence; specialty differences are driven by encounter context, with surgical specialties leading interpersonal qualities and psychiatry lowest. Cluster analysis identifies four physician archetypes, from \"Uniform High\" (33.8%, high across traits) to \"Uniform Low\" (22.6%, low across traits). This map of LLM-derived physician traits exposes how LLMs read the U.S. clinical workforce. Pending clinical validation, it opens future research on fairness, bias, and how LLM-mediated provider search shapes patient choice."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in npj Digital Medicine"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03997","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:40Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-07T02:15:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03474","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03474","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03474","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nadeeshan","familyName":"De Silva","name":"De Silva, Nadeeshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Kellogg","name":"Kellogg, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oscar","familyName":"Chaparro","name":"Chaparro, Oscar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Absolute to Relative Code Comprehensibility Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T19:51:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:08:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-06T15:47:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-07T00:56:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automatically predicting code comprehensibility could support tasks such as refactoring and code review. Existing metrics correlate poorly with human comprehension, motivating ML models that predict comprehensibility directly from code and developer features. Prior models predict absolute comprehensibility (AC), a comprehensibility value for an isolated snippet, but perform poorly since AC is a subjective proxy for a complex cognitive process. We propose relative comprehensibility (RC) as an alternative task: given two snippets, predict which is easier to understand or whether they are comparable. We hypothesize RC is easier to learn, since it only requires identifying distinguishing features between snippets rather than estimating absolute values. Using 150 Java snippets and 12,540 human comprehensibility measurements from two prior studies, we compare AC and RC prediction across classical ML models, a CNN, and two LLMs, evaluating both snippet-wise (aggregate) and developer-wise (individual-judgment) predictions. AC models rarely beat simple baselines (at most 33.4\\% average relative improvement), while snippet-wise RC models outperform baselines in 96.8\\% of configurations, with gains up to 159.8\\% consistent across architectures, though developer-wise results are more variable. We surveyed 38 practitioners and found both AC and RC useful, with a stronger preference for RC in comparison-oriented tasks like refactoring and review."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03474","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:50:46Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:50:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-07T02:15:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03434","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03434","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03434","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiying","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Zhiying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raihan","familyName":"Seraj","name":"Seraj, Raihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcos","familyName":"Villagra","name":"Villagra, Marcos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bidhan","familyName":"Roy","name":"Roy, Bidhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Paris: A Decentralized Trained Open-Weight Diffusion Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T18:53:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:06:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-12T03:17:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-13T02:02:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-31T16:40:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-03T00:57:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Paris, the first publicly released diffusion model pre-trained entirely through decentralized computation. Paris demonstrates that high-quality text-to-image generation can be achieved without centrally coordinated infrastructure. Paris is open for research and commercial use. Paris required implementing our Distributed Diffusion Training framework from scratch. The model consists of 8 expert diffusion models (129M-605M parameters each) trained in complete isolation with no gradient, parameter, or intermediate activation synchronization. Rather than requiring synchronized gradient updates across thousands of GPUs, we partition data into semantically coherent clusters where each expert independently optimizes its subset while collectively approximating the full distribution. A lightweight transformer router dynamically selects appropriate experts at inference, achieving generation quality comparable to centrally coordinated baselines. Eliminating synchronization enables training on heterogeneous hardware without specialized interconnects. Empirical validation confirms that Paris's decentralized training maintains generation quality while removing the dedicated GPU cluster requirement for large-scale diffusion models. Paris achieves this using 14$\\times$ less training data and 16$\\times$ less compute than the prior decentralized baseline."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03434","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:49:49Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:49:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-03T02:08:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04281","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04281","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04281","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuangzhi","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Zhuangzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyi","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Hongyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"He","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, He","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinkai","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Qinkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feixiang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Feixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Fu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinru","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Jinru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eduard","familyName":"Shantsila","name":"Shantsila, Eduard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Uazman","familyName":"Alam","name":"Alam, Uazman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alena","familyName":"Shantsila","name":"Shantsila, Alena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wahbi","familyName":"El-Bouri","name":"El-Bouri, Wahbi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gregory Y. H.","familyName":"Lip","name":"Lip, Gregory Y. H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yalin","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Yalin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RetiBridge: Bridging Quantitative Retinal Biomarkers and Qualitative Diagnosis with a Knowledge-Guided Multimodal Large Language Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T16:46:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:01:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-30T05:20:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-31T00:26:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retinal biomarkers captured by color fundus photography and optical coherence tomography provide clinically valuable evidence for both ocular and systemic diseases. Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have shown promise for retinal image interpretation, yet existing ophthalmic models rarely quantify these clinically relevant biomarkers or explicitly translate their measurements into qualitative, evidence-grounded diagnostic conclusions. To address this gap, we introduce RetiBridge, a knowledge-guided multimodal large language model that jointly analyzes color fundus photography (CFP), optical coherence tomography (OCT), and text, explicitly bridging quantitative retinal biomarkers to qualitative clinical sub-inferences and coherent diagnostic conclusions. RetiBridge combines knowledge-guided instruction generation, OCT-biomarker alignment, and supervised multimodal instruction tuning to learn a biomarker-grounded quantitative-to-qualitative diagnostic pathway. Using 15,611 paired CFP-OCT samples from UK Biobank with 31 OCT and 6 CFP biomarkers, we construct the Grounded Ophthalmic Understanding benchmark to evaluate diagnostic classification, report generation quality, and fine-grained clinical quality. Despite using only LoRA-based fine-tuning of a 7B-parameter Qwen2 backbone, RetiBridge outperforms all evaluated open-source 7B and 32B baselines, achieving the highest quantitative accuracy, evidence grounding, coverage completeness, and BERTScore, while surpassing OpenAI o3 on these key biomarker-grounded metrics. Our code and data are released in the RetiBridge repository."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 4 figures, 3 table. Equal contribution: Zhuangzhi Gao and Hongyi Qin. Corresponding author: Yalin Zheng (yzheng@liverpool.ac.uk)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04281","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:10:40Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:10:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-31T02:35:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03314","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03314","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03314","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shucheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shucheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingzhang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Bingzhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Monjurul","familyName":"Karim","name":"Karim, Muhammad Monjurul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kehua","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kehua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Chenxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehrdad","familyName":"Nasri","name":"Nasri, Mehrdad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinhai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yinhai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Camera-Based Sensing to Reasoning: A Comprehensive Review Toward Proactive Vulnerable Road User Safety"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T23:50:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:02:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-25T05:48:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-28T00:19:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/tits.2026.3707876","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Ensuring the safety of vulnerable road users (VRUs), such as pedestrians and cyclists, remains a critical challenge, as conventional infrastructure-based measures are often insufficient in dynamic urban environments. Recent advances in learning-based visual sensing systems have created new opportunities for more adaptive and context-aware VRU protection. This paper presents a comprehensive review of camera-based methods for proactive VRU safety, with a focus on developments over the past five years. Unlike prior surveys that primarily emphasize detection, we organize the literature into three interconnected components: visual perception (detection and classification), motion modeling (tracking and trajectory prediction), and behavior understanding (intent recognition and reasoning). These components form a unified hierarchical pipeline that enables early risk anticipation and timely intervention. Furthermore, this survey systematically incorporates emerging AI paradigms, including Vision Transformers (ViTs), Large Language Models (LLMs), and diffusion models, highlighting their roles in representation learning, uncertainty modeling, and semantic reasoning. Finally, we identify four key challenges specific to VRU safety, including data scarcity, behavioral uncertainty, edge deployment efficiency, and real-world sensing constraints, and discuss corresponding research directions. This work provides a unified foundation for the development of reliable, scalable, and deployable VRU safety systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 4 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03314","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:55Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-28T03:48:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04019","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04019","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04019","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anthony","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Anthony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Simple Policy Gradients for Reasoning with Diffusion Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T03:53:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:47:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-01T03:54:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T01:59:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-23T05:31:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-24T00:25:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion large language models (dLLMs) represent a promising alternative to autoregressive LLMs; however, the lack of effective post-training techniques, including reinforcement learning (RL), remains a key challenge for dLLMs, especially for downstream applications. Existing approaches often rely on a sequence-level view that requires biased likelihood approximations. In this work, we propose Amortized Group Relative Policy Optimization (AGRPO), a policy gradient algorithm that leverages the Markovian nature of dLLMs, optimizing individual denoising steps rather than full sequences. Our approach improves alignment between the trained policy and the inference process and also admits efficient, unbiased gradient updates via a novel timestep estimation scheme. We demonstrate AGRPO's effectiveness on different math and reasoning tasks, achieving absolute accuracy gains of +59.4\\% and +69.7\\% on Countdown and Sudoku over the base LLaDA model, exceeding comparable methods such as diffu-GRPO. Code is available at https://github.com/probablyabot/agrpo."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages. ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04019","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:13Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-24T02:11:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04487","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04487","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04487","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Willa","familyName":"Potosnak","name":"Potosnak, Willa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Malcolm","familyName":"Wolff","name":"Wolff, Malcolm","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengfei","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Mengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruijun","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Ruijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tatiana","familyName":"Konstantinova","name":"Konstantinova, Tatiana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dmitry","familyName":"Efimov","name":"Efimov, Dmitry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael W.","familyName":"Mahoney","name":"Mahoney, Michael W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boris","familyName":"Oreshkin","name":"Oreshkin, Boris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kin G.","familyName":"Olivares","name":"Olivares, Kin G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Forking-Sequences: Statistically and Computationally Efficient Multi-Horizon Forecasting with Reduced Volatility"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T04:51:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:14:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-16T00:31:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-17T01:12:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-17T20:50:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-19T01:09:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-08T17:43:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-01-09T01:56:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-10T03:07:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-13T00:14:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While accuracy is a critical requirement for time series forecasting, an equally important desideratum is reasonable forecast volatility across forecast creation dates (FCDs). Even highly accurate models can produce erratic revisions between FCDs, undermining trust and disrupting downstream decision-making. To improve the volatility of forecast revisions, state-of-the-art models like MQCNN, MQT, and SPADE employ a powerful yet underexplored neural network architectural design: forking-sequences. This architectural design jointly encodes and decodes the entire time series across all FCDs, producing an entire multi-horizon forecast grid in a single forward pass. This approach contrasts with conventional neural forecasting methods that process FCDs independently, generating only a single multi-horizon forecast per forward pass. In this work, we formalize the forking-sequences design and motivate its broader adoption by introducing a metric for quantifying excess volatility in forecast revisions and by providing theoretical and empirical analysis. We theoretically motivate three key benefits of forking-sequences: (i) reduced forecast volatility through ensembling; (ii) gradient variance reduction, improving the statistical efficiency of the training procedure; and (iii) improved inference computational efficiency. We validate the benefits of forking-sequences compared to baseline window-sampling on the M-series benchmark, using 16 datasets from the M1, M3, M4, and Tourism competitions. We observe median sCRPS improvements across datasets of 46.2%, 49.3%, 28.6%, 24.7%, and 6.4% for RNN, LSTM, CNN, Transformer, and State Space-based architectures, respectively. We then show that forecast ensembling during inference can reduce median forecast volatility by 13.2%, 13.0%, 10.9%, 10.2%, and 11.2% for these respective models trained with forking-sequences, while maintaining accuracy."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in Transactions on Machine Learning Research"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04487","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:15:43Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:15:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-13T01:48:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03595","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03595","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03595","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haikang","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Haikang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Po-Nien","familyName":"Kung","name":"Kung, Po-Nien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nanyun","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Nanyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Decoupling Task-Solving and Output Formatting in LLM Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T00:52:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:16:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-10T05:48:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-13T00:17:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.18653/v1/2026.acl-long.764","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly adept at solving complex problems, such as mathematical reasoning and automatic evaluation. However, performance often degrades when prompts intertwine task instructions with rigid formatting requirements. This entanglement creates competing goals for the model, hindering its reasoning capabilities. To address this, we introduce Deco-G, a decoding framework that explicitly decouples format adherence from problem solving. Deco-G delegates format adherence to a separate Format Estimation Module (FEM), which performs probabilistic lookahead to estimate future format compliance rate and reweighs token probabilities, allowing the LLM to focus solely on task resolution. To make this approach both practical and efficient, we introduce three key innovations: instruction-aware distillation, a flexible trie-building algorithm, and HMM state pruning. Experiments across mathematical reasoning, event argument extraction, and LLM-as-a-judge demonstrate that Deco-G constantly gains over prompting or structured generation baselines, with guaranteed format compliance. We release our code at https://github.com/haikangdeng/deco-g."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Update to the latest ACL published version and add a link to the released code"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03595","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T02:53:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:53:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-13T01:48:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04547","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04547","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04547","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seunghyeon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Seunghyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taesun","familyName":"Yeom","name":"Yeom, Taesun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinho","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jinho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wonpyo","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Wonpyo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyuyeun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Kyuyeun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaeho","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jaeho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Activation Quantization of Vision Encoders Needs Prefixing Registers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T07:27:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:18:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T06:33:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:27:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-28T12:54:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-01T02:26:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-19T12:18:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-20T00:55:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-08T06:28:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-09T00:24:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large pretrained vision encoders are central to multimodal intelligence, powering applications from on-device vision processing to vision-language models. Since these applications often demand real-time processing of massive visual data, reducing the inference cost of vision encoders is critical. Quantization offers a practical path, but it remains challenging even at 8-bit precision due to so-called outliers. In this work, we propose $\\textit{RegCache}$, a training-free algorithm that mitigates outliers in large-scale pretrained vision encoders and serves as a plug-in module that can be applied on top of other quantization methods. RegCache introduces outlier-prone yet semantically meaningless prefix tokens to the vision encoder, which prevent other tokens from having outliers. Notably, we observe that outliers in vision encoders behave differently from those in language models, motivating two technical innovations: middle-layer prefixing and token deletion. Experimental results show that our method consistently improves quantized model performance across various vision encoders, particularly in extremely low-bit regimes (e.g., 4-bit)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ECCV 2026. Code: https://github.com/spbob0418/RegCache"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04547","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:11Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-09T02:02:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04628","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04628","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04628","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunhao","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yunhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Mingyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maoguo","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Maoguo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Bruzzone","name":"Bruzzone, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Spatial-Spectral-Frequency Interactive Network for Multimodal Remote Sensing Classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:33:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:22:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-09T08:24:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-10T00:37:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-07T15:42:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-08T00:58:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.patcog.2026.114309","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep learning-based methods have achieved significant success in remote sensing Earth observation data analysis. Numerous feature fusion techniques address multimodal remote sensing image classification by integrating global and local features. However, these techniques often struggle to extract structural and detail features from heterogeneous and redundant multimodal images. With the goal of introducing frequency domain learning to model key and sparse detail features, this paper introduces the spatial-spectral-frequency interaction network (S$^2$Fin), which integrates pairwise fusion modules across the spatial, spectral, and frequency domains. Specifically, we propose a high-frequency sparse enhancement transformer that employs sparse spatial-spectral attention to optimize the parameters of the high-frequency filter. Subsequently, a two-level spatial-frequency fusion strategy is introduced, comprising an adaptive frequency channel module that fuses low-frequency structures with enhanced high-frequency details, and a high-frequency resonance mask that emphasizes sharp edges via phase similarity. In addition, a spatial-spectral attention fusion module further enhances feature extraction at intermediate layers of the network. Experiments on four benchmark multimodal datasets with limited labeled data demonstrate that S$^2$Fin performs superior classification, outperforming state-of-the-art methods. The code is available at https://github.com/HaoLiu-XDU/SSFin."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04628","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T03:19:07Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:19:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-08T02:09:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04520","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04520","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04520","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruohan","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Ruohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoxiong","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Guoxiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xintao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Xintao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Aria: An Agent For Retrieval and Iterative Auto-Formalization via Dependency Graph"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T06:25:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:16:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-02T09:05:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-03T00:37:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate auto-formalization of theorem statements is essential for advancing automated discovery and verification of research-level mathematics, yet remains a major bottleneck for LLMs due to hallucinations, semantic mismatches, and their inability to synthesize new definitions. To tackle these issues, we present Aria (Agent for Retrieval and Iterative Autoformalization), a system for conjecture-level formalization in Lean that emulates human expert reasoning via a two-phase Graph-of-Thought process: recursively decomposing statements into a dependency graph and then constructing formalizations from grounded concepts. To ensure semantic correctness, we introduce AriaScorer, a checker that retrieves definitions from Mathlib for term-level grounding, enabling rigorous and reliable verification. We evaluate Aria on diverse benchmarks. On ProofNet, it achieves 91.6% compilation success rate and 68.5% final accuracy, surpassing previous methods. On FATE-X, a suite of challenging algebra problems from research literature, it outperforms the best baseline with 44.0% vs. 24.0% final accuracy. On a dataset of homological conjectures, Aria reaches 42.9% final accuracy while all other models score 0%."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04520","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:16:31Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:16:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-03T02:13:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04484","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04484","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04484","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amin","familyName":"Banayeeanzade","name":"Banayeeanzade, Amin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ala N.","familyName":"Tak","name":"Tak, Ala N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fatemeh","familyName":"Bahrani","name":"Bahrani, Fatemeh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anahita","familyName":"Bolourani","name":"Bolourani, Anahita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leonardo","familyName":"Blas","name":"Blas, Leonardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emilio","familyName":"Ferrara","name":"Ferrara, Emilio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Gratch","name":"Gratch, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sai Praneeth","familyName":"Karimireddy","name":"Karimireddy, Sai Praneeth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Psychological Steering in LLMs: An Evaluation of Effectiveness and Trustworthiness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T04:49:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:14:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-02T02:33:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-03T00:19:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The ability to control LLMs' emulated emotional states and personality traits is an essential step in enabling rich, human-centered interactions in socially interactive settings. We introduce PsySET, a Psychologically-informed benchmark to evaluate LLM Steering Effectiveness and Trustworthiness across the emotion and personality domains. Our study spans four models from different LLM families paired with various steering strategies, including prompting, fine-tuning, and representation engineering. Our results indicate that prompting is consistently effective but limited in intensity control, whereas vector injections achieve finer controllability while slightly reducing output quality. Moreover, we explore the trustworthiness of steered LLMs by assessing safety, truthfulness, fairness, and ethics, highlighting potential side effects and behavioral shifts. Notably, we observe idiosyncratic effects; for instance, even a positive emotion like joy can degrade robustness to adversarial factuality, lower privacy awareness, and increase preferential bias. Meanwhile, anger predictably elevates toxicity yet strengthens leakage resistance. Our framework establishes the first holistic evaluation of emotion and personality steering, offering insights into its interpretability and reliability for socially interactive applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ACL 2026. Camera-ready version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04484","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:15:38Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:15:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-03T02:13:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04391","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04391","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04391","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saurabh","familyName":"Ranjan","name":"Ranjan, Saurabh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian","familyName":"Odegaard","name":"Odegaard, Brian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Psychological Imagination Networks Show Cross-Population Centrality and Clustering Alignment in Humans That Large Language Models Fail to Replicate"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T23:01:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:08:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-06T19:24:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-10T01:02:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-07T07:07:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-09T01:45:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-10T02:12:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02-11T01:18:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-01T18:39:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-03T00:05:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mental imagery vividness is a stable individual trait, yet whether imagined scenarios share relational structure across human and synthetic large language model (LLM) populations remains unknown. We applied psychological network analysis to vividness ratings from two validated questionnaires: the Vividness of Visual Imagery Questionnaire (VVIQ-2) and the Plymouth Sensory Imagery Questionnaire (PSIQ), across geographically and linguistically distinct human samples (Florida, Poland, and London; total N = 2,743) and six large language models (LLMs; Gemma3-12B/27B, their quantization-aware counterparts, Llama3.3-70B, and Llama4-16x17B). Imagination networks were constructed as regularized partial correlation graphs, with node centrality and community structure compared across populations using Pearson correlations and the Adjusted Rand Index (ARI). Human networks showed robust cross-population centrality correlations for expected influence, strength, and closeness (r = 0.31-0.93), and community detection recovered clusters aligned with VVIQ-2 scene contexts (ARI = 0.27-0.40) and PSIQ sensory modalities (ARI = 0.87-1.0). Betweenness centrality was unstable across all populations, consistent with its sensitivity to individual experiential history. LLMs failed to replicate human network structure: LLM-human centrality correlations were weak and largely non-significant after correction, and most LLM configurations produced degenerate single-cluster topologies (median ARI = 0). This failure was consistent across model architectures, parameter scales (12B-272B), and conversational conditions. We posit that these findings may be driven by human imagination networks reflecting memory organization accumulated through embodied experience, a representational structure that linguistic training alone does not reproduce regardless of model scale and conversational memory."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04391","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:13:21Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:13:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-03T02:13:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04140","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04140","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04140","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zishang","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Zishang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinyi","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Jinyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tingyun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tingyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sihang","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Sihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqing","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Jiaqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoqian","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Zhaoqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuguang","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Shuguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanghua","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Yanghua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Selective Expert Guidance for Effective and Diverse Exploration in Reinforcement Learning of LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T10:38:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:54:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-01T03:04:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-02T00:18:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) has become a widely adopted technique for enhancing the reasoning ability of Large Language Models (LLMs). However, the effectiveness of RLVR strongly depends on the capability of base models. This issue arises because it requires the model to have sufficient capability to perform high-quality exploration, which involves both effectiveness and diversity. Unfortunately, existing methods address this issue by imitating expert trajectories, which improve effectiveness but neglect diversity. To address this, we argue that the expert only needs to provide guidance only at critical decision points rather than the entire reasoning path. Based on this insight, we propose MENTOR: Mixed-policy Expert Navigation for Token-level Optimization of Reasoning, a framework that provides expert guidance only at critical decision points to perform effective and diverse exploration in RLVR. Extensive experiments show that MENTOR enables models capture the essence of expert strategies rather than surface imitation, thereby performing high-quality exploration and achieving superior overall performance. Our code is available online."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04140","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:07:12Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:07:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-02T02:11:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03310","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03310","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03310","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runze","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Runze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaowei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Mingyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Predicting Effects, Missing Distributions: Evaluating LLMs as Human Behavior Simulators in Operations Management"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T20:20:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:02:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-27T08:14:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-30T00:28:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used to simulate human behavior in business, economics, and the social sciences, offering a low-cost complement to laboratory experiments, field studies, and surveys. This paper evaluates how well LLMs replicate human behavior in operations management. Using nine published behavioral-operations experiments, we assess LLM performance along two dimensions: whether LLM-generated data reproduce the original hypothesis-test outcomes, and whether their full response distributions align with human data, measured by Wasserstein distance. We find that LLMs often replicate hypothesis-level effects, suggesting that they can capture salient decision biases and behavioral regularities. However, their response distributions frequently diverge from human data, even for strong proprietary models, with dispersion mismatch playing an important role. We also examine two lightweight mitigation strategies: chain-of-thought prompting and hyperparameter tuning. Both can reduce distributional misalignment, and appropriate tuning can sometimes allow smaller or open-source models to match or outperform larger proprietary systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03310","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:49Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-30T04:07:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03243","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03243","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03243","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiheng","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Yiheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihe","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yihe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Dearing","name":"Dearing, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuping","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Yuping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael E.","familyName":"Papka","name":"Papka, Michael E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiling","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Zhiling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Ranking Before Serving: Low-Latency LLM Serving via Pairwise Learning-to-Rank"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-25T07:26:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:00:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T04:42:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:23:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-26T09:26:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-29T00:35:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.23919/isc.2026.11520485","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Efficient scheduling of large language model (LLM) inference tasks is critical for achieving low latency and high throughput, a challenge that is becoming increasingly acute with the rise of reasoning-capable LLMs whose generation lengths are highly variable. Traditional strategies like First Come, First-Serve (FCFS) often suffer from Head-of-Line (HOL) blocking, where long-running tasks delay shorter ones queued behind them. In this paper, we introduce PARS, a prompt-aware LLM task scheduler that mitigates HOL blocking by approximating shortest-job-first (SJF) scheduling through pairwise ranking with a margin ranking loss. PARS effectively predicts response-length-based task ordering directly from prompts, thereby optimizing scheduling decisions with minimal overhead. In addition, it integrates seamlessly with vLLM, a state-of-the-art LLM serving system, for the research community. Extensive experiments across multiple LLM models and real-world inference use cases, including chat, math, and code generation, demonstrate that PARS significantly reduces latency by up to 15.7x compared to the vLLM default scheduler. Cross-model evaluations demonstrate that our design generalizes effectively, allowing effective scheduling across diverse LLMs without requiring model-specific retraining."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 4 figures. Published in ISC High Performance 2026 Research Paper Proceedings (41st International Conference)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03243","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:09Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-29T01:59:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04773","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04773","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04773","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqi","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jiaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianxiang","familyName":"He","name":"He, Jianxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyuan","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Haoyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifei","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongzhe","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Yongzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiantian","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tiantian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Houde","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Houde","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T12:49:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:31:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-24T11:41:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-25T00:49:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate remarkable capabilities learned from vast corpora, concerns regarding data privacy and safety are receiving increasing attention. LLM unlearning, which aims to remove the influence of specific data while preserving overall model utility, is becoming an important research area. One of the mainstream unlearning classes is optimization-based methods, which achieve forgetting directly through fine-tuning, exemplified by Negative Preference Optimization (NPO). However, NPO's effectiveness is limited by its inherent lack of explicit positive preference signals. Attempts to introduce such signals by constructing preferred responses often necessitate domain-specific knowledge or well-designed prompts, fundamentally restricting their generalizability. In this paper, we shift the focus to the distribution-level, directly targeting the next-token probability distribution instead of entire responses, and derive a novel unlearning algorithm termed \\textbf{Di}stribution \\textbf{P}reference \\textbf{O}ptimization (DiPO). We show that the requisite preference distribution pairs for DiPO, which are distributions over the model's output tokens, can be constructed by selectively amplifying or suppressing the model's high-confidence output logits, thereby effectively overcoming NPO's limitations. We theoretically prove the consistency of DiPO's loss function with the desired unlearning direction. Extensive experiments demonstrate that DiPO achieves a strong trade-off between model utility and forget quality. Notably, DiPO attains the highest forget quality on the TOFU benchmark, and maintains leading scalability and sustainability in utility preservation on the MUSE benchmark."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04773","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:22:42Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:22:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-25T02:18:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04584","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04584","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04584","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fernando","familyName":"López","name":"López, Fernando","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Santosh","familyName":"Kesiraju","name":"Kesiraju, Santosh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jordi","familyName":"Luque","name":"Luque, Jordi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robustness assessment of large audio language models in multiple-choice evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:36:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:19:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-24T15:08:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-25T01:01:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large audio language models (LALMs) have primarily been assessed using a multiple-choice question answering (MCQA) framework. However, subtle changes, such as shifting the order of choices, result in substantially different results. Existing MCQA frameworks do not account for this variability and report a single accuracy number per benchmark or category. We dive into the MCQA evaluation framework and conduct a systematic study spanning three benchmarks (MMAU, MMAR and MMSU) and four models: Audio Flamingo 2, Audio Flamingo 3, Qwen2.5-Omni-7B-Instruct, and Kimi-Audio-7B-Instruct. Our findings indicate that models are sensitive not only to the ordering of choices, but also to the paraphrasing of the question and the choices. Finally, we propose a simpler evaluation protocol and metric that account for subtle variations and provide a more detailed evaluation report of LALMs within the MCQA framework."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in Interspeech 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04584","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:18:04Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:18:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-25T02:18:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04767","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04767","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04767","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wonjun","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Wonjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Galim","name":"Galim, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seunghyuk","familyName":"Oh","name":"Oh, Seunghyuk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minjae","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Minjae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuibai","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shuibai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Coleman","familyName":"Hooper","name":"Hooper, Coleman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuezhou","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yuezhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyung Il","familyName":"Koo","name":"Koo, Hyung Il","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nam Ik","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Nam Ik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kangwook","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Kangwook","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ParallelBench: Understanding the Trade-offs of Parallel Decoding in Diffusion LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T12:41:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:31:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-23T05:37:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-24T00:28:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While most autoregressive LLMs are constrained to one-by-one decoding, diffusion LLMs (dLLMs) have attracted growing interest for their potential to dramatically accelerate inference through parallel decoding. Despite this promise, the conditional independence assumption in dLLMs causes parallel decoding to ignore token dependencies, inevitably degrading generation quality when these dependencies are strong. However, existing works largely overlook these inherent challenges, and evaluations on standard benchmarks (e.g., math and coding) are not sufficient to capture the quality degradation caused by parallel decoding. To address this gap, we first provide an information-theoretic analysis of parallel decoding. We then conduct case studies on analytically tractable synthetic list operations from both data distribution and decoding strategy perspectives, offering quantitative insights that highlight the fundamental limitations of parallel decoding. Building on these insights, we propose ParallelBench, the first benchmark specifically designed for dLLMs, featuring realistic tasks that are trivial for humans and autoregressive LLMs yet exceptionally challenging for dLLMs under parallel decoding. Using ParallelBench, we systematically analyze both dLLMs and autoregressive LLMs, revealing that: (i) dLLMs under parallel decoding can suffer dramatic quality degradation in real-world scenarios, and (ii) current parallel decoding strategies struggle to adapt their degree of parallelism based on task difficulty, thus failing to achieve meaningful speedup without compromising quality. Our findings underscore the pressing need for innovative decoding methods that can overcome the current speed-quality trade-off. We release our benchmark to help accelerate the development of truly efficient dLLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICLR 2026. Project Page: https://parallelbench.github.io"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04767","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:22:33Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:22:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-24T02:27:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04120","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04120","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04120","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengying","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Fengying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shanshan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shanshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lidia S.","familyName":"Chao","name":"Chao, Lidia S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Derek F.","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Derek F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Probing Semantic Alignment, Lexical Invariance, and Syntactic Influence in LLM Metaphor Processing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T09:45:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:53:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T10:49:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T00:43:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) achieve strong performance on metaphor detection and interpretation tasks, yet it remains unclear what such behavioral success reveals about metaphor processing. We present a diagnostic analysis that examines the limits of behavioral evidence by probing three complementary dimensions: semantic attribute alignment, lexical invariance, and syntactic sensitivity. Using geometric probing, we assess whether model-generated interpretations align with reference semantic attributes; through context-varying substitution, we analyze the stability of lexical associations between metaphorical and literal expressions; and via controlled syntactic perturbations, we examine sensitivity in metaphor detection. Our analysis reveals that LLM-generated interpretations can exhibit semantic drift relative to reference attributes; stable lexical anchors persist across contextual conditions, potentially supporting conventional metaphors while biasing novel metaphors requiring contextual integration; and detection performance is sensitive to syntactic irregularities. These findings suggest that strong behavioral performance may reflect heterogeneous underlying signals, highlighting the need for caution when interpreting metaphor benchmarks as evidence of robust, integrated semantic understanding."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ACL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04120","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:06:42Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:06:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-18T02:18:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04212","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04212","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04212","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiquan","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Haiquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quanming","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Quanming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Why Low-Precision Transformer Training Fails: An Analysis on Flash Attention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T14:01:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:57:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T11:14:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:42:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-18T01:36:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-21T00:24:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-14T08:42:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-16T00:53:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The pursuit of computational efficiency has driven the adoption of low-precision formats for training transformer models. However, this progress is often hindered by notorious training instabilities. This paper provides the first mechanistic explanation for a long-standing and unresolved failure case where training with flash attention in low-precision settings leads to catastrophic loss explosion. Our in-depth analysis reveals that the failure is not a random artifact but caused by two intertwined phenomena: the emergence of similar low-rank representations within the attention mechanism and the compounding effect of biased rounding errors inherent in low-precision arithmetic. We demonstrate how these factors create a vicious cycle of error accumulation that corrupts weight updates, ultimately derailing the training dynamics. To validate our findings, we introduce a minimal modification to the flash attention that mitigates the bias in rounding errors. This simple change stabilizes the training process, confirming our analysis and offering a practical solution to this persistent problem. Code is available at https://github.com/ucker/why-low-precision-training-fails."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04212","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:08:58Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:08:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-16T04:06:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03584","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03584","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03584","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaoyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianzhi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tianzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zhenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqian","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Ziqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maozheng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Maozheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juntong","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Juntong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pooyan","familyName":"Fazli","name":"Fazli, Pooyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FrameOracle: Learning What to See and How Much to See in Videos"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T00:24:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:15:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-30T05:18:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:27:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-13T07:46:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-16T00:27:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language models (VLMs) advance video understanding but operate under tight computational budgets, making performance dependent on selecting a small, high-quality subset of frames. Existing frame sampling strategies, such as uniform or fixed-budget selection, fail to adapt to variations in content density or task complexity. To address this, we present FrameOracle, a lightweight, plug-and-play module that predicts both (1) which frames are most relevant to a given query and (2) how many frames are needed. FrameOracle is trained via a curriculum that progresses from weak proxy signals, such as cross-modal similarity, to stronger supervision with FrameOracle-41K, the first large-scale VideoQA dataset with validated keyframe annotations specifying minimal sufficient frames per question. Extensive experiments across five VLMs and six benchmarks show that FrameOracle reduces 16-frame inputs to an average of 10.4 frames without accuracy loss. When starting from 64-frame candidates, it reduces inputs to 13.9 frames on average while improving accuracy by 1.5%, achieving state-of-the-art efficiency-accuracy trade-offs for scalable video understanding."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2026. Project page: https://people-robots.github.io/frameoracle/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03584","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:53:28Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:53:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-16T04:06:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03896","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03896","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03896","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyi","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Xiaoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muzhi","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Muzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Canyu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Canyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yating","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yating","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyi","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Haoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunhua","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Chunhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GAE: Unleashing Physical Potential of VLM with Generalizable Action Expert"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T18:33:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:40:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-11T03:17:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-12T00:23:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language models demonstrate strong reasoning and planning abilities, yet grounding these predictions into precise robot actions remains a central challenge. Existing Vision-Language-Action methods typically entangle reasoning and action generation, leading to limited generalization. We propose Generalizable Action Expert (GAE), a task-agnostic model that converts sparse geometric plans into dense robot actions. Our approach introduces a sparse geometric interface: the VLM predicts sparse 3D waypoints representing high-level intention, while GAE maps these waypoints together with real-time point cloud observations to continuous action trajectories. GAE is pretrained on a large-scale pointcloud-trajectory dataset comprising 150k trajectories from both simulation and real-world robots. To further improve efficiency and generalization, we introduce an Action Pre-training, Pointcloud Fine-tuning (APPF) scheme that decouples learning action dynamics from geometry grounding. After pretraining, GAE is frozen and reused across downstream tasks, requiring only lightweight fine-tuning of the VLM to produce the sparse interface. Experiments show that our method achieves strong performance and generalization across diverse visual domains, camera viewpoints, and natural language instructions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03896","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:11Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-12T02:19:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04567","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04567","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04567","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weishuo","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Weishuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanbo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yanbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiyuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Muhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GILT: An LLM-Free, Tuning-Free Graph Foundational Model for In-Context Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:09:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:19:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-22T05:06:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-25T00:24:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-10T08:33:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-11T00:39:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) are powerful tools for processing relational data but often struggle to generalize to unseen graphs, giving rise to the development of Graph Foundational Models (GFMs). However, current GFMs are challenged by the extreme heterogeneity of graph data, where each graph can possess a unique feature space, label set, and topology. To address this, two main paradigms have emerged. The first leverages Large Language Models (LLMs), but is fundamentally text-dependent, thus struggles to handle the numerical features in vast graphs. The second pre-trains a structure-based model, but the adaptation to new tasks typically requires a costly, per-graph tuning stage, creating a critical efficiency bottleneck. In this work, we move beyond these limitations and introduce \\textbf{G}raph \\textbf{I}n-context \\textbf{L}earning \\textbf{T}ransformer (GILT), a framework built on an LLM-free and tuning-free architecture. GILT introduces a novel token-based framework for in-context learning (ICL) on graphs, reframing classification tasks spanning node, edge and graph levels in a unified framework. This mechanism is the key to handling heterogeneity, as it is designed to operate on generic numerical features. Further, its ability to understand class semantics dynamically from the context enables tuning-free adaptation. Comprehensive experiments show that GILT achieves stronger few-shot performance with significantly less time than LLM-based or tuning-based baselines, validating the effectiveness of our approach. Our code is available at: https://github.com/yiming421/inductnode/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted as an oral presentation at the GFM @ ICML 2026 Workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04567","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:40Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-11T02:25:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03520","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03520","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03520","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kartik","familyName":"Pandit","name":"Pandit, Kartik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sourav","familyName":"Ganguly","name":"Ganguly, Sourav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arnesh","familyName":"Banerjee","name":"Banerjee, Arnesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaahin","familyName":"Angizi","name":"Angizi, Shaahin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arnob","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Arnob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Certifiable Safe RLHF: Semantic Grounding and Fixed Penalty Constraint Optimization for Safer LLM Alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T21:24:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:11:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-10T17:21:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-11T01:10:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Ensuring safety is a foundational requirement for large language models (LLMs). Achieving an appropriate balance between enhancing the utility of model outputs and mitigating their potential for harm is a complex and persistent challenge. Contemporary approaches frequently formalize this problem within the framework of Constrained Markov Decision Processes (CMDPs) and employ established CMDP optimization techniques. However, these methods exhibit two notable limitations. First, their reliance on reward and cost functions renders performance highly sensitive to the underlying scoring mechanism, which must capture semantic meaning rather than being triggered by superficial keywords. Second, CMDP-based training entails tuning dual-variable, a process that is both computationally expensive and does not provide any provable safety guarantee for a fixed dual variable that can be exploitable through adversarial jailbreaks. To overcome these limitations, we introduce Certifiable Safe-RLHF (CS-RLHF) that introduces a cost model trained on a large-scale corpus to assign semantically grounded safety scores. In contrast to the lagrangian-based approach, CS-RLHF adopts a rectified penalty-based formulation. This design draws on the theory of exact penalty functions in constrained optimization, wherein constraint satisfaction is enforced directly through a suitably chosen penalty term. With an appropriately scaled penalty, feasibility of the safety constraints can be guaranteed at the optimizer, eliminating the need for dual-variable updates. Empirical evaluation demonstrates that CS-RLHF outperforms state-of-the-art LLM model responses rendering at-least 5 times efficient against nominal and jail-breaking prompts"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03520","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:51:55Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:51:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-11T02:25:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04514","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04514","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04514","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rachneet","familyName":"Kaur","name":"Kaur, Rachneet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nishan","familyName":"Srishankar","name":"Srishankar, Nishan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sumitra","familyName":"Ganesh","name":"Ganesh, Sumitra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manuela","familyName":"Veloso","name":"Veloso, Manuela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ChartAgent: A Multimodal Agent for Visually Grounded Reasoning in Complex Chart Question Answering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T06:05:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:16:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-07T06:02:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-08T01:21:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-08T20:02:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-10T00:09:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent multimodal LLMs have shown promise in chart-based visual question answering, but their performance declines sharply on unannotated charts-those requiring precise visual interpretation rather than relying on textual shortcuts. To address this, we introduce ChartAgent, a novel agentic framework that explicitly performs visual reasoning directly within the chart's spatial domain. Unlike textual chain-of-thought reasoning, ChartAgent iteratively decomposes queries into visual subtasks and actively manipulates and interacts with chart images through specialized actions such as drawing annotations, cropping regions (e.g., segmenting pie slices, isolating bars), and localizing axes, using a library of chart-specific vision tools to fulfill each subtask. This iterative reasoning process closely mirrors human cognitive strategies for chart comprehension. ChartAgent achieves state-of-the-art accuracy on the ChartBench and ChartX benchmarks, surpassing prior methods by up to 16.07% absolute gain overall and 17.31% on unannotated, numerically intensive queries. Furthermore, our analyses show that ChartAgent is (a) effective across diverse chart types, (b) achieves the highest scores across varying visual and reasoning complexity levels, and (c) serves as a plug-and-play framework that boosts performance across diverse underlying LLMs. Our work is among the first to demonstrate visually grounded reasoning for chart understanding using tool-augmented multimodal agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ACL 2026 (Main Conference). Also presented as an oral paper at the NeurIPS 2025 Multimodal Algorithmic Reasoning Workshop (https://marworkshop.github.io/neurips25/)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04514","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:16:22Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:16:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-10T02:30:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04195","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04195","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04195","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Puzhen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Puzhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuyang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhan","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liqiu","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Liqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Constructing coherent spatial memory in LLM agents through graph rectification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T13:27:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:57:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-08T23:11:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-10T00:15:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Given a map description through global traversal navigation instructions, an LLM can often infer the implicit spatial layout and answer user queries by providing shortest paths. However, such context-dependent querying becomes incapable as environments grow larger, motivating the need for incremental map construction that builds a complete topological graph from stepwise observations. We propose LLM-MapRepair, a framework for LLM-driven construction and map repair, designed to detect, localize, and correct structural inconsistencies in incrementally constructed navigation graphs. Our contributions include a Version Control mechanism for graph construction, an Edge Impact Score for repair prioritization, and a cleaned variant of the MANGO benchmark tailored for LLM-driven map construction and repair. We evaluate the framework on four evaluation settings: a synthetic per-component ablation (gpt-4.1, n=20 seeds per cell), a cross-vendor sweep over seven LLMs from OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google on both synthetic and TextWorld procedurally-generated text-adventure games, a repair-stage evaluation on all 42 cleaned-MANGO games with non-zero residual conflicts (534 conflicts; three vendors x three modes plus two non-LLM references), and an end-to-end natural-text deployment on Chapters 16-17 of Dream of the Red Chamber. On the DRC deployment, LLM-MapRepair achieves 94.3% node recall (+8.6 pp over direct LLM mapping) and 88.2% edge recall (+55.8 pp), using GPT-4.1; the recall improvements come with predicted node and edge counts that are roughly 4x the ground-truth counts (Table 4), reflecting the discretization-driven over-generation trade-off we discuss in the Limitations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04195","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:08:33Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:08:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-10T02:30:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03844","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03844","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03844","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarah C.","familyName":"Lotspeich","name":"Lotspeich, Sarah C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abbey","familyName":"Collins","name":"Collins, Abbey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian J.","familyName":"Wells","name":"Wells, Brian J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashish K.","familyName":"Khanna","name":"Khanna, Ashish K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph","familyName":"Rigdon","name":"Rigdon, Joseph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucy D'Agostino","familyName":"McGowan","name":"McGowan, Lucy D'Agostino","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On Using Large Language Models to Enhance Clinically-Driven Missing Data Recovery Algorithms in Electronic Health Records"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Applications (stat.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T15:45:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-10T00:07:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1093/jamiaopen/ooag080","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Objective: Electronic health records (EHR) data are prone to missingness and errors. Previously, we devised an \"enriched\" chart review protocol where a \"roadmap\" of auxiliary diagnoses (anchors) was used to recover missing values in EHR data (e.g., a diagnosis of impaired glycemic control might imply that a missing hemoglobin A1c value would be considered unhealthy). Still, chart reviews are expensive and time-intensive, which limits the number of patients whose data can be reviewed. Now, we investigate the accuracy and scalability of a roadmap-driven algorithm, based on ICD-10 codes (International Classification of Diseases, 10th revision), to mimic expert chart reviews and recover missing values. Materials and Methods: In addition to the clinicians' original roadmap from our previous work, we consider new versions that were iteratively refined using large language models (LLM) in conjunction with clinical expertise to expand the list of auxiliary diagnoses. Using chart reviews for 100 patients from the EHR at an extensive learning health system, we examine algorithm performance with different roadmaps. Using the larger study of $1000$ patients, we applied the final algorithm, which used a roadmap with clinician-approved additions from the LLM. Results: The algorithm recovered as much, if not more, missing data as the expert chart reviewers, depending on the roadmap. Discussion: Clinically-driven algorithms (enhanced by LLM) can recover missing EHR data with similar accuracy to chart reviews and can feasibly be applied to large samples. Extending them to monitor other dimensions of data quality (e.g., plausability) is a promising future direction."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03844","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T02:59:49Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:59:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-10T02:30:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03511","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03511","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03511","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad Mohaiminul","familyName":"Islam","name":"Islam, Mohammad Mohaiminul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishabh","familyName":"Anand","name":"Anand, Rishabh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David R.","familyName":"Wessels","name":"Wessels, David R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Friso","familyName":"de Kruiff","name":"de Kruiff, Friso","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thijs P.","familyName":"Kuipers","name":"Kuipers, Thijs P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rex","familyName":"Ying","name":"Ying, Rex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Clara I.","familyName":"Sánchez","name":"Sánchez, Clara I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sharvaree","familyName":"Vadgama","name":"Vadgama, Sharvaree","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Georg","familyName":"Bökman","name":"Bökman, Georg","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erik J.","familyName":"Bekkers","name":"Bekkers, Erik J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Platonic Transformers: A Solid Choice For Equivariance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T20:51:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:10:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:09:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:13:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-02T22:28:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-04T00:13:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While widespread, Transformers lack inductive biases for geometric symmetries common in science and computer vision. Existing equivariant methods often sacrifice the efficiency and flexibility that make Transformers so effective through complex, computationally intensive designs. We introduce the Platonic Transformer to resolve this trade-off. By defining attention relative to reference frames from the Platonic solid symmetry groups, our method induces a principled weight-sharing scheme. This enables combined equivariance to continuous translations and Platonic symmetries, while preserving the exact architecture and computational cost of a standard Transformer. Furthermore, we show that this attention is formally equivalent to a dynamic group convolution, which reveals that the model learns adaptive geometric filters and enables a highly scalable, linear-time convolutional variant. Across diverse benchmarks in computer vision (CIFAR-10), 3D point clouds (ScanObjectNN), and molecular property prediction (QM9, OMol25), the Platonic Transformer achieves competitive performance by leveraging these geometric constraints at no additional cost."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03511","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:51:42Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:51:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-04T03:28:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03259","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03259","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03259","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoonjeon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Yoonjeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Doohyuk","familyName":"Jang","name":"Jang, Doohyuk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eunho","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Eunho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Verifying Meta-Awareness via Predictive Rewards in Reasoning Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-26T14:05:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:00:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-31T02:55:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-02T00:56:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent research on reasoning models explores the meta-awareness of language models, including their ability to determine optimal thinking duration, recognize knowledge boundaries, and structure concept-level thinking. While current large reasoning models depend solely on answer-based verification, we show that adding meta-awareness objectives leads to significant performance gains over models without such meta-knowledge. MAPR (Meta-Awareness via Predictive Reward) utilizes a self-generated task of predicting rollout statistics - specifically length, pass-rate, and concepts used - allowing for verification against the actual statistics. Furthermore, by leveraging this self-predictive capability, the model can regulate its reasoning behavior by i) filtering out trivial or unsolvable prompts, ii) reducing lengthy generations that tend to be incorrect, and iii) generating hints relevant to the problem. The results are inspiring: MAPR yields significant improvements in both accuracy and training efficiency on various reasoning benchmarks. More specifically, our method can speed up GRPO training by over 1.28x to reach the same performance, and achieve 83.18% gain in accuracy on AIME25, and a 13.04% average gain over six mathematics benchmarks. The code is publicly available at https://github.com/akatigre/MAPR-RL."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"accepted to ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03259","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:32Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:53:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03415","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03415","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03415","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditya","familyName":"Thimmaiah","name":"Thimmaiah, Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiyang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jayanth","familyName":"Srinivasa","name":"Srinivasa, Jayanth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyi Jessy","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Junyi Jessy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Milos","familyName":"Gligoric","name":"Gligoric, Milos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLMs Lean on Priors, Not Programming Language Semantics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T18:23:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:05:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T03:28:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:22:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-29T17:06:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-01T01:15:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent work asks whether large language models (LLMs) condition their reasoning on explicit rules rather than statistical regularities from pretraining. Program execution provides a canonical instance: formal semantics define behavior through symbolic transition rules that can be systematically altered under distribution shift. We investigate whether LLMs can condition their reasoning on formal semantics through program execution and introduce PLSemanticsBench, pairing featherweight C programs with two semantic systems -- small-step operational semantics and K semantics -- and probing four capabilities: composing rules for final states, selecting rules when state is unmutated, sustaining such conditioning over long traces, and following supplied rules under novel semantics. To decouple semantic reasoning from syntactic familiarity, we redefine familiar operators to induce symbol-meaning conflict and introduce novel symbols defined only through the supplied rules, and stress-test models on Human-Written, LLM-Translated, and Fuzzer-Generated splits with increasing structural complexity. Across 11 frontier LLMs, strong final-state accuracy under standard semantics (up to 90%) drops sharply -- by as much as 40--60% points -- under semantic mutations and increasing structural complexity. Only a handful of models achieve non-zero long-horizon conditioning accuracy, and even the best systems reach just 35%. Together, these results suggest that contemporary LLMs often rely on pretrained lexical associations rather than systematically conditioning on supplied formal rules. PLSemanticsBench is publicly available at https://EngineeringSoftware.github.io/PLSemanticsBench."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03415","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:49:21Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:49:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-01T02:33:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04704","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04704","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04704","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taoyuze","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Taoyuze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengyu","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Fengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chu","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeffrey","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Jeffrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongzhan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Dongzhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yingheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bram","familyName":"Hoex","name":"Hoex, Bram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhicheng","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Zhicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AtomWorld: A Benchmark for Evaluating Spatial Reasoning in Large Language Models on Crystalline Materials"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T11:17:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:27:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T04:08:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:23:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-22T05:18:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-23T01:20:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-28T12:47:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-29T01:02:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have shown promising potential in scientific research, enabling tasks ranging from knowledge retrieval to property prediction. Existing science benchmarks mainly focus on perceptual or knowledge-based tasks, largely ignoring the modelling tasks, a fundamental starting point for any real scientific research. For materials science, constructing and manipulating atomic structures is one of the most creative and least automated steps. In this work, we introduce AtomWorld, a benchmark designed to evaluate the abilities of LLMs on structure modifications. The benchmark includes ten fundamental actions under four widely used modelling categories, enabling verifiable evaluation metrics. We find that Claude Opus 4.6 generally performs the best. While the success rate decreases markedly with increasing modelling complexity, with particularly low success rates (below 12\\% for rotation) for operations involving complex spatial relations. Our results suggest that contemporary LLMs are better suited as copilots for materials structure modelling rather than fully unsupervised autonomous scientific agents. Beyond evaluation, AtomWorld also serves as a testbed and playground for developing future structure-aware models, including reinforcement learning and agentic approaches."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04704","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:21:00Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:21:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-29T02:57:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03559","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03559","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03559","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeya","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zeya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianing","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Jianing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaxing","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Yaxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Toby Jia-Jun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Toby Jia-Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tianshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PrivacyMotiv: Vulnerability-Centered Persona Journeys for Empathic Privacy Reviews in UX Design"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T23:14:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:14:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-20T18:22:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-22T00:04:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-26T20:35:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-28T00:12:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3800645.3813014","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"UX professionals routinely conduct design reviews, yet privacy concerns are often overlooked, not only due to limited tools, but more fundamentally from low intrinsic motivation, driven by limited privacy knowledge, weak empathy for unexpectedly affected users, and low autonomy in identifying harms. We present PrivacyMotiv, an LLM-powered system that generates vulnerability-centered personas, persona journey stories, and traceable design diagnoses grounded in lo-fi user flows to support privacy-oriented UX design review. In a within-subjects study with professional UX practitioners (N=16), PrivacyMotiv significantly improved empathy, intrinsic motivation, and perceived usefulness, with participants identifying 59% more privacy issues and proposing 70% more redesign solutions compared to self-proposed methods. This work contributes empirical insight into motivational barriers in privacy-aware UX and a structured, narrative-driven approach for integrating privacy review into early-stage UX practice."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"33 pages, 17 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03559","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T02:52:52Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:52:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-28T02:53:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03827","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03827","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03827","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueyang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xueyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangming","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yangming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guiyao","familyName":"Tie","name":"Tie, Guiyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongchao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yongchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guowen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Guowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duanfeng","familyName":"Chu","name":"Chu, Duanfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Pan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lichao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Lichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LIBERO-PRO: Towards Robust and Fair Evaluation of Vision-Language-Action Models Beyond Memorization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T14:56:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:36:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-25T08:44:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-26T01:36:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LIBERO has emerged as a widely adopted benchmark for evaluating Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models; however, its current training and evaluation settings are problematic, often leading to inflated performance estimates and preventing fair model comparison. To address these issues, we introduce LIBERO-PRO, an extended LIBERO benchmark that systematically evaluates model performance under reasonable perturbations across four dimensions: manipulated objects, initial states, task instructions, and environments. Experimental results reveal that, although existing models achieve over 90% accuracy under the standard LIBERO evaluation, their performance collapses to 0.0% under our generalized setting. Crucially, this discrepancy exposes the models' reliance on rote memorization of action sequences and environment layouts from the training set, rather than genuine task understanding or environmental perception. For instance, models persist in executing grasping actions when the target object is replaced with irrelevant items, and their outputs remain unchanged even when given corrupted instructions or even messy tokens. These findings expose the severe flaws in current evaluation practices, and we call on the community to abandon misleading methodologies in favor of robust assessments of model generalization and comprehension. Our code is available at: https://github.com/Zxy-MLlab/LIBERO-PRO."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages,7 figures, 0 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03827","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:59:24Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:59:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-26T04:04:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03271","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03271","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03271","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zi","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Zi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhiyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyang","familyName":"Shang","name":"Shang, Haoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulin","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Yulin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingqing","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Qingqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huadi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Huadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peizhao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Peizhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haibo","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Haibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Decision Potential Surface: A Theoretical and Practical Approximation of Large Language Model Decision Boundary"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-27T07:42:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-21T09:16:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-22T00:43:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Decision boundary, the subspace of inputs where a machine learning model assigns equal classification probabilities to two classes, is pivotal in revealing core model properties and interpreting behaviors. While analyzing the decision boundary of large language models (LLMs) has attracted increasing attention recently, constructing it for mainstream LLMs remains computationally infeasible due to the enormous sequence-level output spaces and the autoregressive nature of LLMs. To address this issue, in this paper we propose Decision Potential Surface (DPS), a new notion for analyzing the properties of LLM decisions. DPS is derived from the confidence in distinguishing different classes for each input, which naturally captures the potential of the decision boundary. We prove that the zero-height isohypse in DPS is equivalent to the decision boundary of an LLM, with enclosed regions representing decision regions. By leveraging DPS, for the first time in the literature, we propose a practical decision boundary approximation algorithm, namely K-DPS, which only requires only K finite sequence samples to approximate an LLM's decision boundary with negligible error. We theoretically derive the upper bounds for the absolute error, expected error, and the error concentration between K-DPS and the ideal DPS, demonstrating that such errors can be traded off against sampling times."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Source code: https://github.com/liangzid/DPS"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03271","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:50Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-22T02:29:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03589","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03589","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03589","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ankit","familyName":"Bhardwaj","name":"Bhardwaj, Ankit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ananth","familyName":"Balashankar","name":"Balashankar, Ananth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lakshminarayanan","familyName":"Subramanian","name":"Subramanian, Lakshminarayanan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FieldFormer: Locality-Aware Transformers for Spatio-Temporal Modeling on Sparse Sensor Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T00:37:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:16:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-18T18:07:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-20T00:05:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Spatio-temporal sensor data in real-world systems is often sparse, noisy, and irregular, making latent field reconstruction fundamentally underconstrained. Under extreme sparsity, multiple physically plausible fields may remain consistent with the same observations, requiring models to rely on inductive biases about locality, transport, and spatial regularity. In such regimes, reliable reconstruction is concentrated around the observational support induced by the sensor network, making sensor-space modeling a more identifiable objective than unconstrained global field recovery.\n We introduce FieldFormer, a mesh-free transformer architecture for locality-aware sensor-space modeling in persistent sensor networks. For each query, FieldFormer aggregates local evidence using learnable velocity-scaled offsets that adapt neighborhood geometry to spatio-temporal dependencies. Neighborhoods are constructed as fixed maximal sparse contexts over nearby sensors and bounded temporal windows, enabling stable and scalable inference under extreme sparsity. A local transformer encoder integrates neighborhood information, while a coordinate-based neural field formulation supports mesh-free prediction.\n We evaluate FieldFormer on five synthetic and real-world benchmarks, including anisotropic heat diffusion, shallow-water dynamics, atmospheric transport, and pollution monitoring datasets. Results show that locality-aware reconstruction provides strong advantages when local domains of dependence remain observed, enabling FieldFormer to consistently outperform state-of-the-art baselines on sparse sensor-space prediction tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03589","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:53:35Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:53:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-20T02:45:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04309","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04309","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04309","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dung V.","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Dung V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hieu M.","familyName":"Vu","name":"Vu, Hieu M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nhi Y.","familyName":"Pham","name":"Pham, Nhi Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tan M.","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Tan M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Activation Steering with a Feedback Controller"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T18:05:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:03:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-27T06:33:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-30T00:29:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-16T04:44:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-19T00:29:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Controlling the behaviors of large language models (LLM) is fundamental to their safety alignment and reliable deployment. However, existing steering methods are primarily driven by empirical insights and lack theoretical performance guarantees. In this work, we develop a control-theoretic foundation for activation steering by showing that popular steering methods correspond to the proportional (P) controllers, with the steering vector serving as the feedback signal. Building on this finding, we propose Proportional-Integral-Derivative (PID) Steering, a principled framework that leverages the full PID controller for activation steering in LLMs. The proportional (P) term aligns activations with target semantic directions, the integral (I) term accumulates errors to enforce persistent corrections across layers, and the derivative (D) term mitigates overshoot by counteracting rapid activation changes. This closed-loop design yields interpretable error dynamics and connects activation steering to classical stability guarantees in control theory. Moreover, PID Steering is lightweight, modular, and readily integrates with state-of-the-art steering methods. Extensive experiments across multiple LLM families and benchmarks demonstrate that PID Steering consistently outperforms existing approaches, achieving more robust and reliable behavioral control. The code is publicly available at: https://github.com/dungnvnus/pid-steering"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages in the main text. ICLR2026 Poster"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04309","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:11:21Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:11:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-19T04:48:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03879","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03879","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03879","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruishi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ruishi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brandon","familyName":"Paulsen","name":"Paulsen, Brandon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Umang","familyName":"Mathur","name":"Mathur, Umang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prateek","familyName":"Saxena","name":"Saxena, Prateek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adversarial Agent Collaboration for Correctness Improvements of C to Safe Rust Translation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"D.2; I.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T17:08:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:39:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-08T09:32:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-09T02:22:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-17T11:10:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-19T01:07:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Translating C to memory-safe languages, like Rust, prevents critical memory safety vulnerabilities that are prevalent in legacy C software. Even with recent LLM-based and tool-augmented translators, the resulting Rust code frequently diverges from the C source on inputs absent from the test suite used during translation; this correctness gap on unseen inputs remains a dominant obstacle to reliable, automatic C-to-Rust translation. In this work, we present ACToR (Adversarial C To Rust), a simple LLM-agent loop that closes this gap by adversarially searching for inputs on which the translation diverges from the C source, and using them to drive subsequent refinements. Inspired by GANs, ACToR pits a translator agent against a discriminator agent that collaborate to iteratively refine the Rust translation. On each iteration, the translator agent synthesizes and refines a Rust translation to pass an existing suite of tests, and then the discriminator agent finds new failing tests by constructing and refining a differential fuzzer over the C and Rust binaries. Across 63 real-world command-line C utilities, with an average size of 473 lines of code and the longest reaching thousands of lines in size, ACToR achieves over 90% test pass rate with zero human intervention. The improvement holds across seven agent-LLM configurations on our micro-benchmark, indicating that the loop is largely independent of the choice of underlying translator and LLM. Compared to a non-adversarial, coverage-driven test-generation baseline, ACToR improves correctness by up to 36.7%. When applied on top of one recent translator, C2SaferRust, ACToR further improves the validation pass rate by 16.6%."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03879","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:00:45Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:00:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-19T04:48:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04682","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04682","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04682","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chanjoo","familyName":"Jung","name":"Jung, Chanjoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaehyung","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jaehyung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TiTok: Transfer Token-level Knowledge via Contrastive Excess to Transplant LoRA"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T10:47:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:26:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-28T14:47:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:43:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-14T06:31:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-15T00:34:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are widely applied in real world scenarios, yet fine-tuning them comes with significant computational and storage costs. Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) methods such as LoRA mitigate these costs; however, the adapted parameters are dependent on the base model and cannot be transferred across different backbones. One way to address this issue is through knowledge distillation, but its effectiveness inherently depends on training data. Recent work such as TransLoRA avoids this by generating synthetic data; nevertheless, this adds complexity since it requires training an additional discriminator model. In this paper, we propose TiTok, a new framework that enables effective LoRA Transplantation through Token-level knowledge transfer. Specifically, TiTok captures task-relevant information through a token-wise contrastive excess between a source model with and without LoRA. This excess highlights informative tokens and enables selective filtering of synthetic data, all without additional models or overhead. Through experiments on three benchmarks across multiple transfer settings, we demonstrate that TiTok is consistently effective, achieving average performance gains of +4~10% compared to baselines overall."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04682","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:20:27Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:20:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-15T02:35:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03992","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03992","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03992","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jehyeok","familyName":"Yeon","name":"Yeon, Jehyeok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Isha","familyName":"Chaudhary","name":"Chaudhary, Isha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gagandeep","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Gagandeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantitative Certification of Agentic Tool Selection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T01:50:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:46:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-13T14:28:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-14T01:11:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in agentic systems, where a fundamental task is mapping user intents to relevant external tools. Errors in tool selection can have severe outcomes, such as unauthorized data access, even without modifying the agent's underlying model. Existing evaluations measure performance on curated, benign benchmarks. However, a pipeline's behavior in deployment depends on the tool pool the agent actually encounters, which in open registries is shaped by third parties. We introduce LLMCert-T, the first statistical framework that returns \\textbf{high-confidence upper bounds on the probability that a tool-selection pipeline satisfies a declared safety specification under a realistic tool distribution}. LLMCert-T models tool-selection evaluation as a Bernoulli estimation problem, drawing inserted-tool sequences from a distribution that the safety specification fixes. To evaluate robustness against realistic deployment conditions, we instantiate this distribution as a stochastic process that generates inserted-tool sequences round by round, conditioning each round on the agent's selection in the previous round. LLMCert-T aggregates the per-trial outcomes into a one-sided Clopper-Pearson upper bound on the probability that the specification is satisfied. By returning this bound as a certificate with statistical guarantees over the inserted-tool sequence distribution, LLMCert-T makes safety claims intuitive, actionable, and comparable across models, retrievers, mitigations, and registry policies. Across popular BFCL and OpenAPI tool pools, LLMCert-T shows that current LLM agents remain fragile under Distractor Selection and Top-N Saturation specifications: their certified correctness upper bounds drop to approximately 20\\%, far below their clean-pool lower bounds."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03992","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:33Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-14T02:50:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04265","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04265","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04265","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohsen","familyName":"Hariri","name":"Hariri, Mohsen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amirhossein","familyName":"Samandar","name":"Samandar, Amirhossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Hinczewski","name":"Hinczewski, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vipin","familyName":"Chaudhary","name":"Chaudhary, Vipin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Don't Pass@k: A Bayesian Framework for Large Language Model Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T16:14:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:00:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-24T05:18:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-25T01:17:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-18T23:24:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-12T00:09:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-12T01:55:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-13T00:24:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pass$@k$ is widely used to report the reasoning performance of LLMs, but it often produces unstable and potentially misleading rankings, especially when the number of trials (samples) is limited and computational resources are constrained. We present a principled Bayesian evaluation framework that replaces Pass$@k$ and average accuracy over $N$ trials (avg$@N$) with posterior estimates of a model's underlying success probability and credible intervals, yielding stable rankings and a transparent decision rule for differences. Evaluation outcomes are modeled as categorical (not just 0/1) with a Dirichlet prior, giving closed-form expressions for the posterior mean and uncertainty of any weighted rubric and enabling the use of prior evidence when appropriate. Theoretically, under a uniform prior, the Bayesian posterior mean is order-equivalent to average accuracy (Pass$@1$), explaining its empirical robustness while adding principled uncertainty. Empirically, in simulations with known ground-truth success rates and on AIME'24/'25, HMMT'25, and BrUMO'25, the posterior-based procedure achieves faster convergence and greater rank stability than Pass$@k$ and recent variants, enabling reliable comparisons at far smaller sample counts. The framework clarifies when observed gaps are statistically meaningful (non-overlapping credible intervals) versus noise, and it naturally extends to graded, rubric-based evaluations. Together, these results recommend replacing Pass$@k$ for LLM evaluation and ranking with a posterior-based, compute-efficient protocol that unifies binary and non-binary evaluation while making uncertainty explicit. Source code is available at https://github.com/mohsenhariri/scorio"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"OpenReview (ICLR 2026): https://openreview.net/forum?id=PTXi3Ef4sT"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04265","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:10:17Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:10:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-13T02:57:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04056","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04056","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04056","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rijha","familyName":"Safdar","name":"Safdar, Rijha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danyail","familyName":"Mateen","name":"Mateen, Danyail","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Syed Taha","familyName":"Ali","name":"Ali, Syed Taha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wajahat","familyName":"Hussain","name":"Hussain, Wajahat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"QuiLL: An LLM-Based Vulnerability Assessment Framework for the Wild"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T06:34:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:49:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-12T17:38:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-13T01:25:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated exceptional progress in multiple domains of software engineering including software vulnerability detection. Using LLMs to automate vulnerability detection in the wild is an important and relatively under-explored problem. In this paper we propose QuiLL, the first comprehensive evaluation framework for real-world vulnerability detection. Our solution consists of an end-to-end pipeline that draws together cutting-edge LLM optimization techniques and strategies specifically catering to the complexities of real-world vulnerability detection. Our specific contributions include (i) diverse prompt designs for vulnerability detection and reasoning (ii) a real-world vector data store constructed from the National Vulnerability Database to provide dynamic in-context learning, and (iii) a novel scoring metric which quantifies accuracy and reasoning quality of model predictions. QuiLL enables researchers to easily and systematically benchmark and compare the vulnerability detection capabilities of various LLMs and assess their readiness for deployment in actual code production pipelines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04056","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:05:06Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:05:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-13T02:57:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03853","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03853","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03853","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuanhang","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Chuanhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dejing","familyName":"Dou","name":"Dou, Dejing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"UGround: Towards Unified Visual Grounding with Unrolled Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T15:56:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:37:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-10T12:37:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-11T01:49:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-15T12:45:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-17T01:54:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-12T10:05:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-13T00:54:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present UGround, a \\textbf{U}nified visual \\textbf{Ground}ing paradigm that dynamically selects intermediate layers across \\textbf{U}nrolled transformers as ``mask as prompt,'' diverging from the prevailing pipeline that leverages the fixed last hidden layer as ``\\texttt{} as prompt.'' UGround addresses two primary challenges posed by the prevailing paradigm: (1) its reliance on the fixed last hidden layer, which sequentially amplifies cumulative errors arising from layer-by-layer propagation without intermediate correction, and (2) its use of \\texttt{} as a prompt, which implicitly projects textual embeddings into visual space without explicit spatial cues (e.g., coordinates). Central to UGround is Policy-Prompted Masking, which comprises two key components: Stochastic Skip Connection (SSC) and Mask as Prompt (MasP). SSC is a reinforcement learning policy that, via stochastic sampling, allows each \\texttt{} token to slide across unrolled transformer layers, enabling dynamic layer selection at which it connects to the vision model (e.g., SAM) in a skip-connection fashion. Given the selected hidden layer, MasP uses the similarity map derived from the \\texttt{} token and image tokens as a soft logit mask to prompt SAM for mask generation, offering explicit spatial cues through its activation regions. To validate the effectiveness of UGround, we, for the first time, have unified visual grounding within a single framework from an attribute perspective, spanning from traditional refer expression segmentation to newly proposed reasoning segmentation, single-target to multi-target, positive query to false premise (empty target). All code and models are publicly available at https://github.com/rui-qian/UGround."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This work has been accepted to ICML 2026, please refer to https://github.com/rui-qian/UGround"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03853","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:00:03Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:00:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-13T02:57:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04233","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04233","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04233","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yuqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junheng","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Junheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qitian","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Qitian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PAINET: A Principled Efficient Transformer for 3D Dynamics Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T14:48:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:58:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-09T23:49:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-12T01:00:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modeling 3D dynamics is a fundamental problem in multi-body systems across scientific and engineering domains and has important practical implications in object trajectory prediction and simulation. While recent GNN-based approaches have achieved strong performance by enforcing geometric symmetries, encoding high-order features or incorporating neural-ODE mechanics, they typically depend on explicitly observed structures and inherently fail to capture the unobserved interactions that are crucial to complex physical behaviors and dynamics mechanism. In this paper, we propose PAINET, a principled SE(3)-equivariant transformer for learning all-pair interactions in multi-body systems. The model comprises: (1) a novel physics-inspired attention network derived from the minimization trajectory of an energy function, and (2) a parallel decoder that preserves equivariance while enabling efficient inference. Empirical results on diverse real-world benchmarks, including human motion capture, molecular dynamics, and large-scale protein simulations, show that PAINET consistently outperforms recently proposed models, yielding 4.7% to 41.5% error reductions in 3D dynamics prediction with comparable computation costs in terms of time and memory. Our codes, baseline models and datasets are available at https://github.com/Icarus1411/PAINET."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04233","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:09:30Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:09:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-12T05:03:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04142","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04142","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04142","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"En","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, En","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Turning Drift into Constraint: Robust Reasoning Alignment in Non-Stationary Multi-Stream Environments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T10:42:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:54:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-02T06:27:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-05T00:23:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-11T11:36:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-12T02:04:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper identifies a critical yet underexplored challenge in reasoning alignment from multiple multi-modal large language models (MLLMs): In non-stationary environments, the diverse reasoning distributions of source models often evolve unpredictably, transmitting systematic biases and drift to the target model. To address this, we formulate multi-source reasoning alignment as a constraint satisfaction problem under concept drift theory. We propose Autonomous Preference Optimization (APO), a novel framework that treats inter-model divergences not as noise, but as dynamic negative constraints. APO operates via a two-stage protocol: first, supervised bootstrapping projects the target model into the capability union of source models; second, constraint-aware optimization synthesizes a consistent consensus manifold by explicitly suppressing drifting trajectories via a multi-negative Plackett-Luce objective. Extensive experiments on chest X-ray interpretation demonstrate that our 7B model achieves superior robustness, outperforming even proprietary source models in average accuracy. Furthermore, we release CXR-MAX, a large-scale benchmark comprising 170,982 reasoning trajectories from seven large-scale MLLMs to facilitate research on reasoning alignment under drift. Code and data are available at: https://github.com/XiaoyuYoung/APO."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04142","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:07:15Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:07:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-12T05:03:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03761","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03761","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03761","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard A.","familyName":"Dubniczky","name":"Dubniczky, Richard A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bertalan","familyName":"Borsos","name":"Borsos, Bertalan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tamas","familyName":"Bisztray","name":"Bisztray, Tamas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Norbert","familyName":"Tihanyi","name":"Tihanyi, Norbert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"You Have Been LaTeXpOsEd: A Systematic Analysis of Information Leakage in Preprint Archives Using Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T10:03:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:30:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T10:09:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-12T01:59:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The widespread use of preprint repositories such as arXiv has accelerated the communication of scientific results but also introduced overlooked security risks. Beyond PDFs, these platforms provide unrestricted access to original source materials, including LaTeX sources, auxiliary code, figures, and embedded comments. In the absence of sanitization, submissions may disclose sensitive information that adversaries can harvest using open-source intelligence. In this work, we present the first large-scale security audit of preprint archives, analyzing more than 1.2 TB of source data from 100,000 arXiv submissions. We introduce LaTeXpOsEd, a four-stage framework that integrates pattern matching, logical filtering, traditional harvesting techniques, and large language models (LLMs) to uncover hidden disclosures within non-referenced files and LaTeX comments. To evaluate LLMs' secret-detection capabilities, we introduce LLMSec-DB, a benchmark on which we tested 25 state-of-the-art models. Our analysis uncovered thousands of PII leaks, GPS-tagged EXIF files, publicly available Google Drive and Dropbox folders, editable private SharePoint links, exposed GitHub and Google credentials, and cloud API keys. We also uncovered confidential author communications, internal disagreements, and conference submission credentials, exposing information that poses serious reputational risks to both researchers and institutions. We urge the research community and repository operators to take immediate action to close these hidden security gaps. To support open science, we release all scripts and methods from this study but withhold sensitive findings that could be misused, in line with ethical principles. The source code and related material are available at the project website https://github.com/LaTeXpOsEd"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03761","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:57:48Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:57:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-12T05:03:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04166","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04166","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04166","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco Edoardo","familyName":"Palma","name":"Palma, Marco Edoardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pooja","familyName":"Rani","name":"Rani, Pooja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harald C.","familyName":"Gall","name":"Gall, Harald C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi Language Models for On-the-Fly Syntax Highlighting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T11:48:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:55:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-04T18:14:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-06T00:03:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Syntax highlighting is a critical feature in modern software development environments, enhancing code readability and developer productivity. However, delivering accurate highlighting in real time remains challenging for online and web-based development tools due to strict time and memory constraints on backend services. These systems must serve highlights rapidly and frequently, even when code is partially valid or invalid. This has led to on-the-fly syntax highlighting, where visual annotations are generated just before content is served, often at high request rates and under incomplete input conditions. To meet these demands efficiently, state-of-the-art models use deep learning to learn the behavior of brute-force syntax highlighting resolvers, tools that are easy to implement but too slow for production. Through the Deep Abstraction process, brute-force strategies are encoded into fast statistical models that achieve both high accuracy and low-latency inference. Despite their success, such models face key challenges: they support only one programming language per model, require large datasets from slow brute-force generators, and involve resource-intensive training. In multi-language environments, this means maintaining multiple independent models, increasing system complexity and operational cost. This work addresses these issues by introducing a unified model capable of highlighting up to six mainstream programming languages, reducing deployment complexity by a factor of six and improving performance on unseen languages. A novel normalization technique significantly enhances model generalization, while few-shot learning experiments show that a small number of oracle samples can replace large datasets, minimizing dependence on brute-force generators. Combined, these innovations enable efficient, scalable, and cost-effective syntax highlighting across diverse programming languages."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04166","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:07:50Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:07:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-06T02:14:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04236","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04236","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04236","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shikun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shikun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kam Woh","familyName":"Ng","name":"Ng, Kam Woh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wonbong","familyName":"Jang","name":"Jang, Wonbong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiadong","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Jiadong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junlin","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Junlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haozhe","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Haozhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiannis","familyName":"Douratsos","name":"Douratsos, Yiannis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan C.","familyName":"Pérez","name":"Pérez, Juan C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijian","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Zijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Phung","name":"Phung, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Xiang","name":"Xiang, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan-Manuel","familyName":"Pérez-Rúa","name":"Pérez-Rúa, Juan-Manuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scaling Sequence-to-Sequence Generative Neural Rendering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T15:03:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:59:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-02T15:22:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T03:08:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-03T04:02:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-05T00:49:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Kaleido, a family of generative models designed for photorealistic, unified object- and scene-level neural rendering. Kaleido operates on the principle that 3D can be regarded as a specialised sub-domain of video, expressed purely as a sequence-to-sequence image synthesis task. Through a systemic study of scaling sequence-to-sequence generative neural rendering, we introduce key architectural innovations that enable our model to: i) perform generative view synthesis without explicit 3D representations; ii) generate any number of 6-DoF target views conditioned on any number of reference views via a masked autoregressive framework; and iii) seamlessly unify 3D and video modelling within a single decoder-only rectified flow transformer. Within this unified framework, Kaleido leverages large-scale video data for pre-training, which significantly improves spatial consistency and reduces reliance on scarce, camera-labelled 3D datasets -- all without any architectural modifications. Kaleido sets a new state-of-the-art on a range of view synthesis benchmarks. Its zero-shot performance substantially outperforms other generative methods in few-view settings, and, for the first time, matches the quality of per-scene optimisation methods in many-view settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published at ICLR 2026. Project Page: https://shikun.io/projects/kaleido"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04236","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:09:34Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:09:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-05T04:00:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04214","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04214","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04214","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xia","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Xia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luhui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Luhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ye","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuoran","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Zhuoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Teaching LLM to be Persuasive: Reward-Enhanced Policy Optimization for Alignment from Heterogeneous Rewards"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T14:08:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:58:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T14:15:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:38:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-29T16:53:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-30T01:00:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We deploy large language models (LLMs) as business development (BD) agents for persuasive price negotiation in online travel agencies (OTAs). The agent must follow a multi-stage Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) and strict guardrails (no over-promising and no hallucinations), while remaining human-like and effective over long, multi-turn dialogues. We propose Reward-Enhanced Policy Optimization (REPO), a reinforcement learning post-training method that combines heterogeneous rewards: a preference-trained reward model (RM), an LLM-as-a-judge (RJ) for nuanced behaviors (e.g., emotional value and SOP compliance), and rule-based reward functions (RF) (mainly regex-based) for deterministic checks on numerics, formatting, and guardrails. In expert consensus evaluation (three human experts; 30 online conversations and 45 curated bad cases), REPO improves average dialogue rating to 4.63 (+0.33 over GRPO) and raises the share of conversations with at least one excellent response to 66.67% (+23.34 pp over GRPO), while achieving a 93.33% bad-case fix rate with 75.56% clean fixes. In a production A/B test on 9,653 real customer conversations (vs. an intent-driven dialogue system), REPO improves response rate by +12.14 pp and task success rate by +5.94 pp (p\u0026lt;0.001)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"accepted by ACL 2026 indusry track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04214","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:09:02Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:09:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-30T02:19:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04573","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04573","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04573","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoqiang","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Haoqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhe","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikki Lijing","familyName":"Kuang","name":"Kuang, Nikki Lijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicklas","familyName":"Majamaki","name":"Majamaki, Nicklas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Navdeep","familyName":"Jaitly","name":"Jaitly, Navdeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi-An","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yi-An","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lianhui","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Lianhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LaDiR: Latent Diffusion Enhances LLMs for Text Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:15:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:19:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T17:58:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:03:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T07:01:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:35:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-11T22:01:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-15T01:09:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-03T09:27:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:38:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-04-23T01:00:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-04-24T00:15:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate their reasoning ability through chain-of-thought (CoT) generation. However, LLM's autoregressive decoding may limit the ability to revisit and refine earlier tokens in a holistic manner, which can also lead to inefficient exploration for diverse solutions. In this paper, we propose LaDiR} (Latent Diffusion Reasoner), a novel reasoning framework that unifies the expressiveness of continuous latent representation with the iterative refinement capabilities of latent diffusion models for an existing LLM. We first construct a structured latent reasoning space using a Variational Autoencoder (VAE) that encodes text reasoning steps into blocks of thought tokens, preserving semantic information and interpretability while offering compact but expressive representations. Subsequently, we utilize a latent diffusion model that learns to denoise a block of latent thought tokens with a blockwise bidirectional attention mask, enabling longer horizon and iterative refinement with adaptive test-time compute. This design, combined with explicit diversity guidance during diffusion inference, enables the generation of multiple diverse reasoning trajectories that explore distinct regions of the latent space, rather than producing repetitive solutions as often occurs in standard autoregressive sampling. We conduct evaluations on a suite of mathematical reasoning, code generation and puzzle planning benchmarks. Empirical results show that LaDiR consistently improves accuracy, diversity, and interpretability over existing autoregressive, diffusion-based, and latent reasoning methods, revealing a new paradigm for text reasoning with latent diffusion."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04573","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":5,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:49Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-24T02:13:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04548","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04548","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04548","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaito","familyName":"Takanami","name":"Takanami, Kaito","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takashi","familyName":"Takahashi","name":"Takahashi, Takashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoshiyuki","familyName":"Kabashima","name":"Kabashima, Yoshiyuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning Linear Regression with Low-Rank Tasks in-Context"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Disordered Systems and Neural Networks (cond-mat.dis-nn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T07:27:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:18:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-23T09:03:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-24T00:36:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In-context learning (ICL) is a key building block of modern large language models, yet its theoretical mechanisms remain poorly understood. It is particularly mysterious how ICL operates in real-world applications where tasks have a common structure. In this work, we address this problem by analyzing a linear attention model trained on low-rank regression tasks. Within this setting, we precisely characterize the distribution of predictions and the generalization error in the high-dimensional limit. Moreover, we find that statistical fluctuations in finite pre-training data induce an implicit regularization. Finally, we identify a sharp phase transition of the generalization error governed by task structure. These results provide a framework for understanding how transformers learn to learn the task structure."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at AISTATS 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04548","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:12Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-24T02:13:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04371","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04371","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04371","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naimeng","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Naimeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arnav","familyName":"Ahuja","name":"Ahuja, Arnav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Georgios","familyName":"Liargkovas","name":"Liargkovas, Georgios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yunan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kostis","familyName":"Kaffes","name":"Kaffes, Kostis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Speculative Actions: A Lossless Framework for Faster Agentic Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T21:28:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:07:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-23T17:58:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-24T01:04:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI agents are increasingly deployed in complex, interactive environments, yet their runtime remains a major bottleneck for training, evaluation, and real-world use. Typical agent behavior unfolds sequentially, with each action requiring an API call that can incur substantial latency. For example, a game of chess between two state-of-the-art agents can take hours. We introduce Speculative Actions, a lossless acceleration framework for general agentic systems. Inspired by speculative execution in microprocessors and speculative decoding in LLM inference, our method uses faster models to predict likely future actions and execute them in parallel, committing only when predictions match. We evaluate speculative actions across gaming, e-commerce, and web search environments, and additionally study a lossy extension in an operating systems setting. Across domains, we achieve up to 55% next-action prediction accuracy, translating into up to 20% latency reductions. Finally, we present a cost-latency analysis that formalizes the tradeoff between speculative breadth and time savings. This analysis enables principled tuning and selective branch launching to ensure that multi-branch speculation delivers practical speedups without prohibitive cost growth."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04371","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:12:52Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:12:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-24T02:13:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04225","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04225","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04225","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yikun","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Yikun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huijia","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Huijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiqiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Weiqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liqing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Liqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianfu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jianfu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Locate-Then-Examine: Grounded Region Reasoning Improves Detection of AI-Generated Images"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.10; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T45","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T14:29:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:58:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-21T18:16:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-23T00:04:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid growth of AI-generated imagery has blurred the boundary between real and synthetic content, raising practical concerns for digital integrity. Vision-language models (VLMs) can provide natural language explanations, but standard one-pass classifiers often miss subtle artifacts in high-quality synthetic images and offer limited grounding in the pixels. We propose Locate-Then-Examine (LTE), a two-stage VLM-based forensic framework that first localizes suspicious regions and then re-examines these crops together with the full image to refine the real vs. AI-generated verdict and its explanation. LTE explicitly links each decision to localized visual evidence through region proposals and region-aware reasoning. To support training and evaluation, we introduce TRACE, a dataset of 20,000 real and high-quality synthetic images with region-level annotations and automatically generated forensic explanations, constructed by a VLM-based pipeline with additional consistency checks and quality control. Across TRACE and multiple external benchmarks, LTE achieves competitive accuracy and improved robustness while providing human-understandable, region-grounded explanations suitable for forensic deployment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 11 figures (including supplementary material)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04225","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:09:18Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:09:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-23T02:22:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03323","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03323","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03323","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ge","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Ge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinbo","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Jinbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiacheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiacheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengfei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Pengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhao","familyName":"Shang","name":"Shang, Yuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huiwen","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Huiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongli","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Hongli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanchun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuanchun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunxin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yunxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing Agentic Textual Graph Retrieval with Synthetic Stepwise Supervision"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T13:27:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:02:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-14T06:40:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-15T00:31:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-22T11:22:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-23T00:45:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Integrating textual graphs into Large Language Models (LLMs) is promising for complex graph-based QA. However, a key bottleneck is retrieving informative yet compact subgraphs that fit the LLM context. Existing retrievers often struggle, relying either on shallow embedding similarity or costly interactive policies that require excessive supervision. To address these challenges, we introduce an agentic textual graph reasoning framework featuring an LLM-based retriever trained with synthetic stepwise supervision. Rather than relying on final answer rewards which often yield sparse and unstable signals, we optimize the retriever by evaluating each step against offline-extracted golden subgraphs. Our approach distills golden subgraphs via a specialized data synthesis pipeline to formulate dense rewards, facilitating a two-stage training scheme that effectively learns the interactive graph exploration policy. Based on extensive experiments on three common datasets in comparison with seven strong baselines, our approach achieves an average improvement of 15.6% in accuracy and 17.2% in F1 score. The advantage is even higher in more complicated multi-hop reasoning tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03323","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:47:08Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:47:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-23T02:22:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04595","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04595","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04595","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulong","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yulong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianxiong","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jianxiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianguo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jianguo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhichao","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Zhichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bojun","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Bojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luziwei","familyName":"Leng","name":"Leng, Luziwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SpikingMamba: Towards Energy-Efficient Large Language Models via Knowledge Distillation from Mamba"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:49:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:20:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-12T08:00:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T00:44:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable performance across tasks but remain energy-intensive due to dense matrix operations. Spiking neural networks (SNNs) improve energy efficiency by replacing dense matrix multiplications with sparse accumulations. Their sparse spike activity enables efficient LLMs deployment on edge devices. However, prior SNN-based LLMs often sacrifice performance for efficiency, and recovering accuracy typically requires full pretraining, which is costly and impractical. To address this, we propose SpikingMamba, an energy-efficient SNN-based LLMs distilled from Mamba that improves energy efficiency with minimal accuracy sacrifice. SpikingMamba integrates two key components: (a) SI-LIF, a signed-integer spiking neuron that preserves semantic polarity through signed multi-level spike representations. (b) A training-exclusive Smoothed Gradient Compensation (SGC) path mitigating quantization loss while preserving spike-driven efficiency. We employ a single-stage distillation strategy to transfer the zero-shot ability of pretrained Mamba and further enhance it via reinforcement learning (RL). Experiments show that SpikingMamba-1.3B achieves a 4.76$\\times$ energy benefit, with only a 4.78\\% zero-shot accuracy gap compared to the original Mamba. The model achieves a further 2.55\\% accuracy improvement after RL, narrowing the performance gap from 4.78\\% to 2.23\\%. Code is available at: https://github.com/HuuYuLong/SpikingMamba ."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04595","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:18:20Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:18:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:50:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04116","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04116","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04116","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziying","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ziying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaqing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yaqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quanming","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Quanming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Searching Meta Reasoning Skeleton to Guide LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T09:36:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:53:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-27T02:52:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-01T01:17:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-28T09:12:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-31T00:29:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-16T14:08:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-17T00:52:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Meta reasoning behaviors work as a skeleton to guide large language model (LLM) reasoning, thus help to improve reasoning performance. However, prior researches implement meta reasoning skeleton with manually designed structure, limiting ability to adapt to query-specific requirement and capture intricate logical dependency among reasoning steps. To deal with the challenges, we represent meta reasoning skeleton with directed acyclic graph (DAG) to unify skeletons proposed in prior works and model intricate logical dependency. Then we propose AutoMR, a framework that searches for query-aware meta reasoning skeleton automatically inspired by automated machine learning (AutoML). Specifically, we construct search space based on DAG representation of skeleton and then formulate the search problem. We design a dynamic skeleton sampling algorithm by expanding meta reasoning skeleton along with reasoning context at inference time. This algorithm can derive any meta reasoning skeleton in search space efficiently and adapt skeleton to evolving base reasoning context, thus enable efficient query-aware skeleton search. We conduct experiments on extensive benchmark datasets. Experimental results show that AutoMR achieves better reasoning performance than previous works broadly."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04116","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:06:37Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:06:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-17T02:18:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03851","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03851","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03851","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiying","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Ruiying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chieh-Jan Mike","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Chieh-Jan Mike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanjie","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yanjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francis Y.","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Francis Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MetaMuse: Algorithm Generation via Creative Ideation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T15:52:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:37:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-16T03:31:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-17T00:23:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Designing system algorithms remains challenging, where the discontinuous nature of the solution space often forces system engineers to rely on generic heuristics at the expense of performance. We study whether LLMs can practically drive algorithm generation, and find that they are biased towards well-known generic designs, rather than making the creative leaps needed to navigate the discontinuous solution space. To address this limitation, we introduce MetaMuse, a framework for creative ideation built on three self-reflection principles: (1) quantifying solution diversity and usefulness in measurable performance space, rather than abstract idea space, (2) steering ideation through external stimuli, rather than internal randomness, and (3) constructing executable solutions using waypoint reasoning, rather than free-form chain-of-thought. Considering two critical online problems at a global cloud provider, extensive evaluations show that MetaMuse can generate high-performing solutions: it reduces cache misses by up to 35.76% in cache replacement and reduces bin usage by up to 30.93% in online bin packing."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03851","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:00:00Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:00:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-17T02:18:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03989","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03989","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03989","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xue-Cheng","familyName":"Tai","name":"Tai, Xue-Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingfeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Lingfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raymond H.","familyName":"Chan","name":"Chan, Raymond H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Mathematical Explanation of Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T01:16:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:45:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-12T14:04:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-14T01:05:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Transformer architecture has revolutionized the field of sequence modeling and underpins the recent breakthroughs in large language models (LLMs). However, a comprehensive mathematical theory that explains its structure and operations remains elusive. In this work, we propose a novel continuous framework that rigorously interprets the Transformer as a discretization of a structured integro-differential equation. Within this formulation, the self-attention mechanism emerges naturally as a non-local integral operator, and layer normalization is characterized as a projection to a time-dependent constraint. This operator-theoretic and variational perspective offers a unified and interpretable foundation for understanding the architecture's core components, including attention, feedforward layers, and normalization. Our approach extends beyond previous theoretical analyses by embedding the entire Transformer operation in continuous domains for both token indices and feature dimensions. This leads to a principled and flexible framework that not only deepens on theoretical insight but also offers new directions for architecture design, analysis, and control-based interpretations. This new interpretation provides a step toward bridging the gap between deep learning architectures and continuous mathematical modeling, and contributes a foundational perspective to the ongoing development of interpretable and theoretically grounded neural network models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03989","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:28Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-14T04:18:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04641","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04641","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04641","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ayan","familyName":"Majumdar","name":"Majumdar, Ayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feihao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Feihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinghui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jinghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaozhen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaozhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating LLMs for Demographic-Targeted Social Bias Detection: A Comprehensive Benchmark Study"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:45:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:23:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T15:04:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T02:02:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-09T11:53:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-10T00:48:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-scale web-scraped text corpora used to train general-purpose AI models often contain harmful demographic-targeted social biases, creating a regulatory need for data auditing and developing scalable bias-detection methods. Although prior work has investigated biases in text datasets and related detection methods, these studies remain narrow in scope. They typically focus on a single content type (e.g., hate speech), cover limited demographic axes, overlook biases affecting multiple demographics simultaneously, and analyze limited techniques. Consequently, practitioners lack a holistic understanding of the strengths and limitations of recent large language models (LLMs) for automated bias detection. In this study, we conduct a comprehensive benchmark study on English texts to assess the ability of LLMs in detecting demographic-targeted social biases. To align with regulatory requirements, we frame bias detection as a multi-label task of detecting targeted identities using a demographic-focused taxonomy. We then systematically evaluate models across scales and techniques, including prompting, in-context learning, and fine-tuning. Using twelve datasets spanning diverse content types and demographics, our study demonstrates the promise of fine-tuned smaller models for scalable detection. However, our analyses also expose persistent gaps across demographic axes and multi-demographic targeted biases, underscoring the need for more effective and scalable detection frameworks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04641","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:19:27Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:19:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-10T02:24:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03638","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03638","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03638","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jialin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lisang","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Lisang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stanley","familyName":"Osher","name":"Osher, Stanley","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wotao","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Wotao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Expressive Power of Implicit Models: Rich Equilibria and Test-Time Scaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Representation Theory (math.RT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T02:49:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:19:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-28T21:40:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:08:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-01T02:46:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:57:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-31T02:07:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-01T00:19:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Implicit models, an emerging model class, compute outputs by iterating a single parameter block to a fixed point. This architecture realizes an infinite-depth, weight-tied network that trains with constant memory, significantly reducing memory needs for the same level of performance compared to explicit models. While it is empirically known that these compact models can often match or even exceed the accuracy of larger explicit networks by allocating more test-time compute, the underlying mechanism remains poorly understood.\n We study this gap through a nonparametric analysis of expressive power. We provide a strict mathematical characterization, showing that a simple and regular implicit operator can, through iteration, progressively express more complex mappings. We prove that for a broad class of implicit models, this process lets the model's expressive power scale with test-time compute, ultimately matching a much richer function class. The theory is validated across four domains: image reconstruction, scientific computing, operations research, and LLM reasoning, demonstrating that as test-time iterations increase, the complexity of the learned mapping rises, while the solution quality simultaneously improves and stabilizes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03638","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:54:46Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:54:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-01T02:31:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04618","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04618","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04618","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qizheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qizheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changran","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Changran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shubhangi","familyName":"Upasani","name":"Upasani, Shubhangi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyuan","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Boyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fenglu","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Fenglu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vamsidhar","familyName":"Kamanuru","name":"Kamanuru, Vamsidhar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jay","familyName":"Rainton","name":"Rainton, Jay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengmeng","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Mengmeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanchen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hanchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Urmish","familyName":"Thakker","name":"Thakker, Urmish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kunle","familyName":"Olukotun","name":"Olukotun, Kunle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agentic Context Engineering: Evolving Contexts for Self-Improving Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:30:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:22:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-29T19:22:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:03:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-29T09:18:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-31T00:55:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM) applications such as agents and domain-specific reasoning increasingly rely on context adaptation: modifying inputs with instructions, strategies, or evidence, rather than weight updates. Prior approaches improve usability but often suffer from brevity bias, which drops domain insights for concise summaries, and from context collapse, where iterative rewriting erodes details over time. We introduce ACE (Agentic Context Engineering), a framework that treats contexts as evolving playbooks that accumulate, refine, and organize strategies through a modular process of generation, reflection, and curation. ACE prevents collapse with structured, incremental updates that preserve detailed knowledge and scale with long-context models. Across agent and domain-specific benchmarks, ACE optimizes contexts both offline (e.g., system prompts) and online (e.g., agent memory), consistently outperforming strong baselines: +10.6% on agents and +8.6% on finance, while significantly reducing adaptation latency and rollout cost. Notably, ACE could adapt effectively without labeled supervision and instead by leveraging natural execution feedback. On the AppWorld leaderboard, ACE matches the top-ranked production-level agent on the overall average and surpasses it on the harder test-challenge split, despite using a smaller open-source model. These results show that comprehensive, evolving contexts enable scalable, efficient, and self-improving LLM systems with low overhead."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026; 32 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04618","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":3,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:18:53Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:18:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-31T04:02:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03904","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03904","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03904","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hangting","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Hangting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinmeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jinmeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"He","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, He","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingchen","familyName":"Zhuge","name":"Zhuge, Mingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dandan","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Dandan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyuan","familyName":"Zha","name":"Zha, Hongyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM as an Algorithmist: Enhancing Anomaly Detectors via Programmatic Synthesis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T19:00:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:40:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-30T05:47:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-31T01:21:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Existing anomaly detection (AD) methods for tabular data usually rely on some assumptions about anomaly patterns, leading to inconsistent performance in real-world scenarios. While Large Language Models (LLMs) show remarkable reasoning capabilities, their direct application to tabular AD is impeded by fundamental challenges, including difficulties in processing heterogeneous data and significant privacy risks. To address these limitations, we propose LLM-DAS, a novel framework that repositions the LLM from a ``data processor'' to an ``algorithmist''. Instead of being exposed to raw data, our framework leverages the LLM's ability to reason about algorithms. It analyzes a high-level description of a given detector to understand its intrinsic weaknesses and then generates detector-specific, data-agnostic Python code to synthesize ``hard-to-detect'' anomalies that exploit these vulnerabilities. This generated synthesis program, which is reusable across diverse datasets, is then instantiated to augment training data, systematically enhancing the detector's robustness by transforming the problem into a more discriminative two-class classification task. Extensive experiments on 36 TAD benchmarks show that LLM-DAS consistently boosts the performance of mainstream detectors. By bridging LLM reasoning with classic AD algorithms via programmatic synthesis, LLM-DAS offers a scalable, effective, and privacy-preserving approach to patching the logical blind spots of existing detectors."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by the Fourteenth International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03904","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:23Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-31T04:02:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03721","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03721","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03721","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leander","familyName":"Girrbach","name":"Girrbach, Leander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stephan","familyName":"Alaniz","name":"Alaniz, Stephan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Genevieve","familyName":"Smith","name":"Smith, Genevieve","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Trevor","familyName":"Darrell","name":"Darrell, Trevor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeynep","familyName":"Akata","name":"Akata, Zeynep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Person-Centric Annotations of LAION-400M: Auditing Bias and Its Transfer to Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T07:51:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:27:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-29T11:37:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-31T00:58:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language models trained on large-scale multimodal datasets show strong demographic biases, but the role of training data in producing these biases remains unclear. A major barrier has been the lack of demographic annotations in web-scale datasets such as LAION-400M. We address this gap by creating person-centric annotations for the full dataset, including over 276 million bounding boxes, perceived gender and race/ethnicity labels, and automatically generated captions. These annotations are produced through validated automatic labeling pipelines combining object detection, multimodal captioning, and finetuned classifiers. Using them, we uncover demographic imbalances and harmful associations, such as the disproportionate linking of men and individuals perceived as Black or Middle Eastern with crime-related and negative content. We also show that a linear fit predicts 60-70% of gender bias in CLIP and Stable Diffusion from direct co-occurrences in the data. Our resources establish the first large-scale empirical link between dataset composition and downstream model bias. Code is available at https://github.com/ExplainableML/LAION-400M-Person-Centric-Annotations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03721","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:56:48Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:56:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-31T04:02:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04428","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04428","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04428","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanhao","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Yuanhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengji","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Shengji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andong","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Andong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youpeng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Youpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A.I.R.: Enabling Adaptive, Iterative, and Reasoning-based Frame Selection For Video Question Answering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T01:51:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:10:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:08:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:13:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-27T02:48:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-30T00:20:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Effectively applying Vision-Language Models (VLMs) to Video Question Answering (VideoQA) hinges on selecting a concise yet comprehensive set of frames, as processing entire videos is computationally infeasible. However, current frame selection methods face a critical trade-off: approaches relying on lightweight similarity models, such as CLIP, often fail to capture the nuances of complex queries, resulting in inaccurate similarity scores that cannot reflect the authentic query-frame relevance, which further undermines frame selection. Meanwhile, methods that leverage a VLM for deeper analysis achieve higher accuracy but incur prohibitive computational costs. To address these limitations, we propose A.I.R., a training-free approach for Adaptive, Iterative, and Reasoning-based frame selection. We leverage a powerful VLM to perform deep, semantic analysis on complex queries, and this analysis is deployed within a cost-effective iterative loop that processes only a small batch of the most high-potential frames at a time. Extensive experiments on various VideoQA benchmarks demonstrate that our approach outperforms existing frame selection methods, significantly boosts the performance of the foundation VLM, and achieves substantial gains in computational efficiency over other VLM-based techniques."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026 Paper"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04428","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:14:15Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:14:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-30T02:11:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04607","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04607","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04607","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Mingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haibo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Haibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Imperative to Declarative: Towards LLM-friendly OS Interfaces for Boosted Computer-Use Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Operating Systems (cs.OS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:14:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:21:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-25T11:29:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-26T00:47:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3767295.3803576","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Computer-use agents (CUAs) powered by large language models (LLMs) have emerged as a promising approach to automating computer tasks, yet they struggle with the existing human-oriented OS interfaces - graphical user interfaces (GUIs). GUIs force LLMs to decompose high-level goals into lengthy, error-prone sequences of fine-grained actions, resulting in low success rates and an excessive number of LLM calls.\n We propose Declarative Model Interface (DMI), an abstraction that transforms existing GUIs into three declarative primitives: access, state, and observation, thereby providing novel OS interfaces tailored for LLM agents. Our key idea is policy-mechanism separation: LLMs focus on high-level semantic planning (policy) while DMI handles low-level navigation and interaction (mechanism). DMI does not require modifying the application source code or relying on application programming interfaces (APIs).\n We evaluate DMI with Microsoft Office Suite (Word, PowerPoint, Excel) on Windows. Integrating DMI into a leading GUI-based agent baseline improves task success rates by 67% and reduces interaction steps by 43.5%. Notably, DMI completes over 61% of successful tasks with a single LLM call."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04607","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T03:18:37Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:18:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-26T02:29:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04601","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04601","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04601","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guochen","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Guochen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luyuan","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Luyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingni","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Qingni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuejian","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Yuejian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhonghai","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhonghai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:06:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:21:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T12:26:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:44:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-25T07:11:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-26T00:33:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The current paradigm of training large language models (LLMs) on public available Web data is becoming unsustainable as high-quality data sources in specialized domains near exhaustion. Federated Learning (FL) emerges as a practical solution for the next generation of AI on a decentralized Web, enabling privacy-preserving collaborative fine-tuning on decentralized private data. While Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) is standard for efficient fine-tuning, its federated application faces a critical bottleneck: communication overhead under heterogeneous network conditions. Structural redundancy in LoRA parameters increases communication costs and causes aggregation conflicts. To address this, we propose FedSRD, a Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose framework for communication-efficient federated LLM fine-tuning. We introduce importance-aware sparsification to reduce the upload parameter count while preserving the structural integrity of LoRA updates. The server aggregates updates in full-rank space to mitigate conflicts, then decomposes the global update into a sparse low-rank format for broadcast, ensuring a symmetrically efficient cycle. We also propose an efficient variant, FedSRD-e, to reduce computational overhead. Experiments on 10 benchmarks show our framework significantly reduces communication costs by up to 90\\% while improving performance on heterogeneous client data."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by WWW 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04601","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:18:29Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:18:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-26T02:29:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04468","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04468","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04468","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asif Mohammed","familyName":"Samir","name":"Samir, Asif Mohammed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad Masudur","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, Mohammad Masudur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Improving IR-based Bug Localization with Semantics-Driven Query Reduction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T03:43:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:13:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-17T23:49:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-19T00:29:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite decades of research, software bug localization remains challenging due to heterogeneous content and inherent ambiguities in bug reports. Existing methods, such as Information Retrieval (IR)-based approaches, often attempt to match source documents to bug reports, overlooking the context and semantics of the source code. On the other hand, Large Language Models (LLMs) (e.g., Transformer models) show promising results in understanding both texts and code. However, they have not yet been adapted well to localize software bugs using bug reports. They could also be data or resource-intensive. To bridge this gap, we propose, IQLoc, a novel approach that capitalizes on the strengths of both IR and LLMs for bug localization. In particular, we leverage the transformer-based model's understanding of code semantics to reason about its suspiciousness and to reformulate search queries and thus enhance bug localization using Information Retrieval. To evaluate IQLoc, we refine the Bench4BL benchmark dataset and extend it by incorporating ~30% more recent bug reports, resulting in a benchmark containing ~7.5K bug reports. We evaluated IQLoc using three performance metrics and compare it against eight baseline techniques. Experimental results demonstrate its superiority, achieving up to 100.40% and 78.08% in MAP, 61.49% and 64.58% in MRR, and 76.98% and 100.90% in HIT@K for the test bug reports with random and time-wise splits, respectively. Moreover, IQLoc improves MAP by 118.70% for bug reports with stack traces, 111.87% for those that include code elements, and 127.45% for those containing only descriptions in natural language. By integrating program semantic understanding into Information Retrieval, IQLoc mitigates several longstanding challenges of traditional IR-based approaches in bug localization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"62 pages, 18 figures, 11 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04468","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:15:16Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:15:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-19T02:54:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04072","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04072","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04072","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingwei","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Xingwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengpu","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Shengpu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minjia","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Minjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoming","familyName":"Huo","name":"Huo, Xiaoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Slow-Fast Policy Optimization: Reposition-Before-Update for LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T07:22:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:50:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T04:24:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:23:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-15T05:57:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-17T01:07:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-17T22:44:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-19T00:26:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) has become central to enhancing reasoning in large language models (LLMs). Yet on-policy algorithms such as Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) often suffer in early training: noisy gradients from low-quality rollouts lead to unstable updates and inefficient exploration. We introduce Slow-Fast Policy Optimization (SFPO), a simple yet efficient framework to address the above limitations via decomposing each step into three stages: a short fast trajectory of inner steps on the same batch, a reposition mechanism to control off-policy drift, and a final slow correction. This reposition-before-update design preserves the objective and rollout process unchanged, making SFPO plug-compatible with existing policy-gradient pipelines. Extensive experiments demonstrate that SFPO consistently improves stability, reduces number of rollouts, and accelerates convergence of reasoning RL training. Specifically, it outperforms GRPO by up to 2.80 points in average on math reasoning benchmarks. It also achieves up to 4.93\\texttimes{} fewer rollouts and an up to 4.19\\texttimes{} reduction in wall-clock time to match GRPO's best accuracy. Project website is available at https://slow-fast-po.github.io/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04072","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:05:30Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:05:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-19T02:54:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04282","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04282","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04282","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Idan"],"givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Kiu","name":"Kiu, Yu","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Organizational","affiliation":[],"name":"Lau","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ge","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Ge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Flexible and Efficient Spatio-Temporal Transformer for Sequential Visual Place Recognition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T16:52:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:01:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-17T03:00:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-18T00:29:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sequential Visual Place Recognition (Seq-VPR) leverages transformers to capture spatio-temporal features effectively. In practice, a transformer-based Seq-VPR model should be flexible to the number of frames per sequence (seq- length), deliver fast inference, and have low memory usage to meet real-time constraints. However, existing approaches prioritize performance at the expense of flexibility and effi- ciency. To address this gap, we propose Adapt-STformer, a Seq-VPR method built around our novel Recurrent Deformable Transformer Encoder (Recurrent-DTE), which uses an iterative recurrent mechanism to fuse information from multiple sequen- tial frames. This design naturally supports variable seq-lengths, fast inference, and low memory usage. Experiments on the Nordland, Oxford, and NuScenes datasets show that Adapt- STformer boosts recall by up to 17% while reducing sequence extraction time by 36% and lowering memory usage by 35% relative to our best comparable baseline. Our code is released at https://ai4ce.github.io/Adapt-STFormer/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04282","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:10:41Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:10:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-18T02:49:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04476","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04476","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04476","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomas","familyName":"Figliolia","name":"Figliolia, Tomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas","familyName":"Alonso","name":"Alonso, Nicholas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishi","familyName":"Iyer","name":"Iyer, Rishi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quentin","familyName":"Anthony","name":"Anthony, Quentin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beren","familyName":"Millidge","name":"Millidge, Beren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Compressed Convolutional Attention: Efficient Attention in a Compressed Latent Space"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T04:24:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:13:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-16T23:36:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-18T00:23:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-headed Attention's (MHA) quadratic compute and linearly growing KV-cache make long-context transformers expensive to train and serve. Prior works such as Grouped Query Attention (GQA) and Multi-Latent Attention (MLA) shrink the cache, speeding decode, but leave compute, which determines prefill and training speed, largely unchanged. We introduce Compressed Convolutional Attention (CCA), a novel attention method which down-projects queries, keys, and values and performs the entire attention operation inside the shared latent space. This simple design dramatically cuts parameters, KV-cache, and FLOPs all at once by the desired compression factor. Because CCA is orthogonal to head-sharing, we combine the two to form Compressed Convolutional Grouped Query Attention (CCGQA), which further tightens the compute-bandwidth Pareto frontier so that users can tune compression toward either FLOP or memory limits without sacrificing quality. Experiments show that CCGQA consistently outperforms both GQA and MLA at equal KV-cache compression on dense and MoE models. Additionally, we show that CCGQA outperforms all other attention methods on MoE models with half the KV-cache of GQA and MLA, achieving an 8x KV-cache compression with no drop in performance compared to standard MHA. CCA and CCGQA also dramatically reduce the FLOP cost of attention which leads to substantially faster training and prefill than existing methods. On H100 GPUs, our fused CCA/CCGQA kernel reduces prefill latency by about 1.7x at a sequence length of 16k relative to MHA, and accelerates backward by about 1.3x."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04476","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:15:27Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:15:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-18T02:49:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04017","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04017","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04017","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sumanth","familyName":"Varambally","name":"Varambally, Sumanth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marshall","familyName":"Fisher","name":"Fisher, Marshall","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jas","familyName":"Thakker","name":"Thakker, Jas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhirui","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Zhirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yasaman","familyName":"Jafari","name":"Jafari, Yasaman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruijia","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Ruijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manas","familyName":"Jain","name":"Jain, Manas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Veeramakali Vignesh","familyName":"Manivannan","name":"Manivannan, Veeramakali Vignesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zachary","familyName":"Novack","name":"Novack, Zachary","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luyu","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Luyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Srikar","familyName":"Eranky","name":"Eranky, Srikar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Salva Rühling","familyName":"Cachay","name":"Cachay, Salva Rühling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taylor","familyName":"Berg-Kirkpatrick","name":"Berg-Kirkpatrick, Taylor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duncan","familyName":"Watson-Parris","name":"Watson-Parris, Duncan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi-An","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yi-An","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rose","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Rose","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Zephyrus: An Agentic Framework for Weather Science"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Atmospheric and Oceanic Physics (physics.ao-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T03:34:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:46:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-16T19:30:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-18T00:10:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foundation models for weather science are pre-trained on vast amounts of structured numerical data and outperform traditional weather forecasting systems. However, these models lack language-based reasoning capabilities, limiting their utility in interactive scientific workflows. Large language models (LLMs) excel at understanding and generating text but cannot reason about high-dimensional meteorological datasets. We bridge this gap by building the first agentic framework for weather science. Our framework includes a Python code-based environment for agents (ZephyrusWorld) to interact with weather data, featuring tools including a WeatherBench 2 dataset indexer, geolocator for geocoding from natural language, weather forecasting, climate simulation capabilities, and a climatology module for querying precomputed climatological statistics (e.g., means, extremes, and quantiles) across multiple timescales. We design Zephyrus, a multi-turn LLM-based weather agent that iteratively analyzes weather datasets, observes results, and refines its approach through conversational feedback loops. We accompany the agent with a new benchmark, ZephyrusBench, with a scalable data generation pipeline that constructs diverse question-answer pairs across weather-related tasks, from basic lookups to advanced forecasting, extreme event detection, and counterfactual reasoning. Experiments on this benchmark demonstrate the strong performance of Zephyrus agents over text-only baselines, outperforming them by up to 44 percentage points in correctness. However, the hard tasks are still difficult even with frontier LLMs, highlighting the challenging nature of our benchmark and suggesting room for future development. Our codebase and benchmark are available at https://github.com/Rose-STL-Lab/Zephyrus."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04017","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:10Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-18T02:49:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03366","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03366","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03366","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harshwardhan","familyName":"Fartale","name":"Fartale, Harshwardhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashish","familyName":"Kattamuri","name":"Kattamuri, Ashish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Raja","name":"Raja, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arpita","familyName":"Vats","name":"Vats, Arpita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ishita","familyName":"Prasad","name":"Prasad, Ishita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akshata Kishore","familyName":"Moharir","name":"Moharir, Akshata Kishore","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Disentangling Recall and Reasoning in Transformer Models through Layer-wise Attention and Activation Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T04:13:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:03:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-13T08:24:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-16T00:33:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformer-based language models excel at both recall (retrieving memorized facts) and reasoning (performing multi-step inference), but whether these abilities rely on distinct internal mechanisms remains unclear. Distinguishing recall from reasoning is crucial for predicting model generalization, designing targeted evaluations, and building safer interventions that affect one ability without disrupting the other.We approach this question through mechanistic interpretability, using controlled datasets of synthetic linguistic puzzles to probe transformer models at the layer, head, and neuron level. Our pipeline combines activation patching and structured ablations to causally measure component contributions to each task type. Across two model families (Qwen and LLaMA), we find that interventions on distinct layers and attention heads lead to selective impairments: disabling identified \"recall circuits\" reduces fact-retrieval accuracy by up to 15\\% while leaving reasoning intact, whereas disabling \"reasoning circuits\" reduces multi-step inference by a comparable margin. At the neuron level, we observe task-specific firing patterns, though these effects are less robust, consistent with neuronal polysemanticity.Our results provide the first causal evidence that recall and reasoning rely on separable but interacting circuits in transformer models. These findings advance mechanistic interpretability by linking circuit-level structure to functional specialization and demonstrate how controlled datasets and causal interventions can yield mechanistic insights into model cognition, informing safer deployment of large language models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03366","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:48:10Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:48:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-16T02:07:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03666","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03666","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03666","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sichao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Sichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinsong","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yinsong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangyuan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Guangyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyuan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Haoyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lifang","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Lifang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingliang","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Jingliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MonitorVLM:A Vision Language Framework for Safety Violation Detection in Mining Operations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T04:46:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:23:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-11T12:33:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-12T00:46:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Industrial accidents, particularly in high-risk domains such as surface and underground mining, are frequently caused by unsafe worker behaviors. Traditional manual inspection remains labor-intensive, error-prone, and insufficient for large-scale, dynamic environments, highlighting the urgent need for intelligent and automated safety monitoring. In this paper, we present MonitorVLM, a novel vision--language framework designed to detect safety violations directly from surveillance video streams. MonitorVLM introduces three key innovations: (1) a domain-specific violation dataset comprising 9,000 vision--question--answer (VQA) samples across 40 high-frequency mining regulations, enriched with augmentation and auxiliary detection cues; (2) a clause filter (CF) module that dynamically selects the Top-$K$ most relevant clauses, reducing inference latency by 13.56\\% while maintaining accuracy; and (3) a behavior magnifier (BM) module that enhances worker regions to improve fine-grained action recognition, yielding additional gains of 3.45% in precision and 8.62% in recall. Experimental results demonstrate that MonitorVLM significantly outperforms baseline vision--language models, achieving improvements of 22.01% in precision, 34.22\\% in recall, and 28.37% in F1 score over the 72B unfine-tuned baseline. A lightweight web-based interface further integrates MonitorVLM into practical workflows, enabling automatic violation reporting with video timestamping. This study highlights the potential of multimodal large models to enhance occupational safety monitoring in mining and beyond."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03666","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:55:27Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:55:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-12T02:25:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04377","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04377","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04377","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiarui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiarui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixiang","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Zixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengming","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Zhengming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel J.","familyName":"Landry","name":"Landry, Samuel J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramgopal R.","familyName":"Mettu","name":"Mettu, Ramgopal R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TCR-EML: Explainable Model Layers for TCR-pMHC Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T21:47:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:07:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-05T23:12:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-09T00:12:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"T cell receptor (TCR) recognition of peptide-MHC (pMHC) complexes is a central component of adaptive immunity, with implications for vaccine design, cancer immunotherapy, and autoimmune disease. While recent advances in machine learning have improved prediction of TCR-pMHC binding, the most effective approaches are black-box transformer models that cannot provide a rationale for predictions. Post-hoc explanation methods can provide insight with respect to the input but do not explicitly model biochemical mechanisms (e.g. known binding regions), as in TCR-pMHC binding. ``Explain-by-design'' models (i.e., with architectural components that can be examined directly after training) have been explored in other domains, but have not been used for TCR-pMHC binding. We propose explainable model layers (TCR-EML) that can be incorporated into protein-language model backbones for TCR-pMHC modeling. Our approach uses prototype layers for amino acid residue contacts drawn from known TCR-pMHC binding mechanisms, enabling high-quality explanations for predicted TCR-pMHC binding. Experiments of our proposed method on large-scale datasets demonstrate competitive predictive accuracy and generalization, and evaluation on the TCR-XAI benchmark demonstrates improved explainability compared with existing approaches."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"[Abstract] Learning Meaningful Representations of Life (LMRL) Workshop at ICLR 2026 (Project Page: https://tcreml.jiarui.li/)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04377","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:13:01Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:13:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-09T02:09:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03929","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03929","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03929","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Campbell","name":"Campbell, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valentin","familyName":"De Bortoli","name":"De Bortoli, Valentin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxin","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Jiaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arnaud","familyName":"Doucet","name":"Doucet, Arnaud","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-Speculative Masked Diffusions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T20:16:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:42:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-06T10:45:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-09T00:38:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present self-speculative masked diffusions, a new class of masked diffusion generative models for discrete data that require significantly fewer function evaluations to generate samples. Standard masked diffusion models predict factorized logits over currently masked positions. A number of masked positions are then sampled, however, the factorization approximation means that sampling too many positions in one go leads to poor sample quality. As a result, many simulation steps and therefore neural network function evaluations are required to generate high-quality data. We reduce the computational burden by generating non-factorized predictions over masked positions. This is achieved by modifying the final transformer attention mask from non-causal to causal, enabling draft token generation and parallel validation via a novel, model-integrated speculative sampling mechanism. This results in a non-factorized predictive distribution over masked positions in a single forward pass. We apply our method to GPT2 scale text modelling and protein sequence generation, finding that we can achieve a ~2x reduction in the required number of network forward passes relative to standard masked diffusion models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"32 pages, 7 figures, 4 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03929","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:02:00Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:02:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-09T02:09:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04284","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04284","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04284","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunghwei","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Yunghwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiming","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Kaiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyue","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weizhi","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Weizhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Doctor-R1: Mastering Clinical Inquiry with Experiential Agentic Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T16:54:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:01:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-06T06:28:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-09T01:24:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-01T08:43:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:05:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The professionalism of a human doctor in outpatient service depends on two core abilities: the ability to make accurate medical decisions and the medical consultation skill to conduct strategic, empathetic patient inquiry. Existing Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable accuracy on medical decision-making benchmarks. However, they often lack the ability to conduct the strategic and empathetic consultation, which is essential for real-world clinical scenarios. To address this gap, we propose Doctor-R1, an AI doctor agent trained to master both of the capabilities by ask high-yield questions and conduct strategic multi-turn inquiry to guide decision-making. Our framework introduces three key components: a multi-agent interactive environment, a two-tiered reward architecture that separately optimizes clinical decision-making and communicative inquiry skills, and an experience repository to ground policy learning in high-quality prior trajectories. We evaluate Doctor-R1 on OpenAI's HealthBench and MAQuE, assessed across multi-facet metrics, such as communication quality, user experience, and task accuracy. Remarkably, Doctor-R1 surpasses state-of-the-art open-source specialized LLMs by a substantial margin with higher parameter efficiency and outperforms powerful proprietary models. Furthermore, the human expert evaluations show that Doctor-R1 achieves superior clinical capability and patient-centric performance, demonstrating the effectiveness of the framework."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04284","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:10:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:10:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:44:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04080","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04080","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04080","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixin","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Zixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian-Wen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qian-Wen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Di","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Di","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xing","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunping","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chunping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PoLi-RL: A Point-to-List Reinforcement Learning Framework for Conditional Semantic Textual Similarity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T07:57:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:51:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-19T05:11:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-20T01:24:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-28T14:59:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:43:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Conditional Semantic Textual Similarity (C-STS) measures the semantic proximity between text segments under a specific condition, thereby overcoming the ambiguity inherent in traditional STS. However, existing methods are largely confined to discriminative models, failing to fully leverage recent breakthroughs in the NLP community involving Large Language Models (LLMs) and Reinforcement Learning (RL). RL is a particularly well-suited paradigm for this task, as it can directly optimize the non-differentiable Spearman ranking metric and guide the reasoning process required by C-STS. Nevertheless, we find that naively applying listwise RL fails to produce meaningful improvements, as the model struggles with complex, coarse-grained reward signals, leading to optimization difficulties. To address this challenge, we introduce PoLi-RL, a novel Point-to-List Reinforcement Learning framework. PoLi-RL employs a two-stage curriculum: it first trains the model with a simple pointwise reward to establish fundamental scoring capabilities, then transitions to a hybrid reward that combines pointwise, pairwise, and listwise objectives to refine the model's ability to discern subtle semantic distinctions. Crucially, we propose an innovative Parallel Slice Ranking Reward (PSRR) mechanism that computes ranking rewards in parallel slices, where each slice consists of completions with the same index from different samples. This provides a precise, differentiated learning signal for each individual completion, enabling granular credit assignment and effective optimization. On the official C-STS benchmark, PoLi-RL achieves a Spearman correlation coefficient of 48.18, establishing a new SOTA for the cross-encoder architecture. As the first work to successfully apply RL to C-STS, our study introduces a powerful paradigm for aligning LLMs for complex, ranking-based conditional judgment tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04080","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:05:42Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:05:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:44:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04067","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04067","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04067","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junxi","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Junxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixi","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Zixi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingyao","familyName":"Ai","name":"Ai, Qingyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiqun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yiqun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingtao","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Jingtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"What Scales in Cross-Entropy Scaling Law?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T07:06:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:50:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-28T08:15:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:33:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The cross-entropy scaling law has long served as a key tool for guiding the development of large language models. It shows that cross-entropy loss decreases in a predictable power-law rate as the model size increases. However, recent evidence indicates that this law breaks down at very large scales: the loss decreases more slowly than expected, which causes significant trouble for developing large language models. In this paper, we hypothesize that the root cause lies in the fact that cross-entropy itself does not truly scale; instead, only one of its hidden components does. To investigate this, we introduce a novel decomposition of cross-entropy into three parts: Error-Entropy, Self-Alignment, and Confidence. We show both theoretically and empirically that this decomposition precisely captures the training dynamics and optimization objectives. Through extensive experiments on multiple datasets and 32 models spanning five orders of magnitude in size, we find that only error-entropy follows a robust power-law scaling, while the other two terms remain largely invariant. Moreover, error-entropy constitutes the dominant share of cross-entropy in small models but diminishes in proportion as models grow larger. This explains why the cross-entropy scaling law appears accurate at small scales but fails at very large ones. Our findings establish the error-entropy scaling law as a more accurate description of model behavior. We believe it will have wide applications in the training, understanding, and future development of large language models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04067","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:05:22Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:05:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:44:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04040","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04040","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04040","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Song","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Song","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laura","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Laura","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaidi","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Kaidi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianlong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Tianlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FaithCoT-Bench: Benchmarking Instance-Level Faithfulness of Chain-of-Thought Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T05:16:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:48:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-28T06:58:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:31:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) increasingly rely on Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting to improve problem-solving and provide seemingly transparent explanations. However, growing evidence shows that CoT often fail to faithfully represent the underlying reasoning process, raising concerns about their reliability in high-risk applications. Although prior studies have focused on mechanism-level analyses showing that CoTs can be unfaithful, they leave open the practical challenge of deciding whether a specific trajectory is faithful to the internal reasoning of the model. To address this gap, we introduce FaithCoT-Bench, a unified benchmark for instance-level CoT unfaithfulness detection. Our framework establishes a rigorous task formulation that formulates unfaithfulness detection as a discriminative decision problem, and provides FINE-CoT (Faithfulness instance evaluation for Chain-of-Thought), an expert-annotated collection of over 1,000 trajectories generated by four representative LLMs across four domains, including more than 300 unfaithful instances with fine-grained causes and step-level evidence. We further conduct a systematic evaluation of eleven representative detection methods spanning counterfactual, logit-based, and LLM-as-judge paradigms, deriving empirical insights that clarify the strengths and weaknesses of existing approaches and reveal the increased challenges of detection in knowledge-intensive domains and with more advanced models. To the best of our knowledge, FaithCoT-Bench establishes the first comprehensive benchmark for instance-level CoT faithfulness, setting a solid basis for future research toward more interpretable and trustworthy reasoning in LLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04040","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:43Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:44:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03605","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03605","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03605","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adel","familyName":"Javanmard","name":"Javanmard, Adel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baharan","familyName":"Mirzasoleiman","name":"Mirzasoleiman, Baharan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vahab","familyName":"Mirrokni","name":"Mirrokni, Vahab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Understanding the Role of Training Data in Test-Time Scaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T01:38:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:17:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-01T22:03:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:26:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Test-time scaling improves the reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs) by allocating extra compute to generate longer Chains-of-Thoughts (CoTs). This enables models to tackle more complex problem by breaking them down into additional steps, backtracking, and correcting mistakes. Despite its strong performance--demonstrated by OpenAI's o1 and DeepSeek R1, the conditions in the training data under which long CoTs emerge, and when such long CoTs improve the performance, remain unclear. In this paper, we study the performance of test-time scaling for transformers trained on an in-context weight prediction task for linear regression. Our analysis provides a theoretical explanation for several intriguing observations: First, at any fixed test error, increasing test-time compute allows us to reduce the number of in-context examples (context length) in training prompts. Second, if the skills required to solve a downstream task are not sufficiently present in the training data, increasing test-time compute can harm performance. Finally, we characterize task hardness via the smallest eigenvalue of its feature covariance matrix and show that training on a diverse, relevant, and hard set of tasks results in best performance for test-time scaling. We confirm our findings with experiments on large, nonlinear transformer architectures."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 5 figures, accepted in ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03605","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:53:58Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:53:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:44:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03253","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03253","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03253","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heyang","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Heyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zexu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Zexu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erxue","familyName":"Min","name":"Min, Erxue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengyi","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Hengyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuaiqiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shuaiqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dawei","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Dawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Solving the Granularity Mismatch: Hierarchical Preference Learning for Long-Horizon LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-26T08:43:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:00:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T07:26:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:46:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) as autonomous agents are increasingly tasked with solving complex, long-horizon problems. Aligning these agents via preference-based offline methods like Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) is a promising direction, yet it faces a critical granularity mismatch. Trajectory-level DPO provides a signal that is too coarse for precise credit assignment, while step-level DPO is often too myopic to capture the value of multi-step behaviors. To resolve this challenge, we introduce Hierarchical Preference Learning (HPL), a hierarchical framework that optimizes LLM agents by leveraging preference signals at multiple, synergistic granularities. While HPL incorporates trajectory- and step-level DPO for global and local policy stability, its core innovation lies in group-level preference optimization guided by a dual-layer curriculum. Our approach first decomposes expert trajectories into semantically coherent action groups and then generates contrasting suboptimal groups to enable preference learning at a fine-grained, sub-task level. Then, instead of treating all preference pairs equally, HPL introduces a curriculum scheduler that organizes the learning process from simple to complex. This curriculum is structured along two axes: the group length, representing sub-task complexity, and the sample difficulty, defined by the reward gap between preferred and dispreferred action groups. Experiments on three challenging agent benchmarks show that HPL outperforms existing state-of-the-art methods. Our analyses demonstrate that the hierarchical DPO loss effectively integrates preference signals across multiple granularities, while the dual-layer curriculum is crucial for enabling the agent to solve a wide range of tasks, from simple behaviors to complex multi-step sequences."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03253","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:24Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:44:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03632","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03632","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03632","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiaxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yucheng","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Yucheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ninghao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ninghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MITS: Enhanced Tree Search Reasoning for LLMs via Pointwise Mutual Information"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T02:30:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:19:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T02:59:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:16:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Tree search has become as a representative framework for test-time reasoning with large language models (LLMs), exemplified by methods such as Tree-of-Thought and Monte Carlo Tree Search. However, it remains difficult to provide instant and reliable quantitative assessments of intermediate reasoning step quality, and extensive path exploration is computationally costly. To address this, we propose Mutual Information Tree Search (MITS), a novel framework that guides reasoning with information-theoretic principles. MITS introduces an effective scoring function based on pointwise mutual information (PMI), which enables step-wise evaluation of reasoning paths and search tree expansion via beam search without expensive look-ahead simulations, achieving superior reasoning performances while maintaining computational efficiency. The framework is complemented by an entropy-based dynamic sampling strategy that adaptively allocates computational resources to uncertain reasoning steps where exploration is most beneficial. For final prediction, MITS employs a weighted voting scheme that combines PMI scores with prediction consensus. Through comprehensive experiments on diverse reasoning benchmarks, MITS consistently surpasses baseline methods, establishing a principled and efficient framework for LLM reasoning. The code is available at https://github.com/plusnli/MITS."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03632","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:54:37Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:54:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-02T03:06:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03495","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03495","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03495","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erik","familyName":"Pautsch","name":"Pautsch, Erik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tanmay","familyName":"Singla","name":"Singla, Tanmay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Parv","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Parv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxin","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Wenxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huiyun","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Huiyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Behnaz","familyName":"Hassanshahi","name":"Hassanshahi, Behnaz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Konstantin","familyName":"Läufer","name":"Läufer, Konstantin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"George K.","familyName":"Thiruvathukal","name":"Thiruvathukal, George K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James C.","familyName":"Davis","name":"Davis, James C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgentHub: A Registry for Discoverable, Verifiable, and Reproducible AI Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T20:18:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:09:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T18:01:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T02:00:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM-based agents are rapidly proliferating, yet the infrastructure for discovering, evaluating, and governing them remains fragmented compared to mature ecosystems like software package registries (e.g., npm) and model hubs (e.g., Hugging Face). Existing efforts typically address naming, distribution, or protocol descriptors, but stop short of providing a registry layer that makes agents discoverable, comparable, and governable under automated reuse. We present AgentHub, a registry layer and accompanying research agenda for agent sharing that targets discovery and workflow integration, trust and security, openness and governance, ecosystem interoperability, lifecycle transparency, and capability clarity with evidence. We describe a reference prototype that implements a canonical manifest with publish-time validation, version-bound evidence records linked to auditable artifacts, and an append-only lifecycle event log whose states are respected by default in search and resolution. We also provide initial discovery results using an LLM-as-judge recommendation pipeline, showing how structured contracts and evidence improve intent-accurate retrieval beyond keyword-driven discovery. AgentHub aims to provide a common substrate for building reliable, reusable agent ecosystems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03495","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:51:18Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:51:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-27T03:15:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03306","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03306","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03306","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuan","familyName":"Kan","name":"Kan, Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James J.","familyName":"Lah","name":"Lah, James J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deqiang","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Deqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Atlas-free Brain Network Transformer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T18:57:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:02:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T21:58:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:09:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current atlas-based approaches to brain network analysis rely heavily on standardized anatomical or connectivity-driven brain atlases. However, these fixed atlases often introduce significant limitations, such as spatial misalignment across individuals, functional heterogeneity within predefined regions, and atlas-selection biases, collectively undermining the reliability and interpretability of the derived brain networks. To address these challenges, we propose a novel atlas-free brain network transformer (atlas-free BNT) that leverages individualized brain parcellations derived directly from subject-specific resting-state fMRI data. Our approach computes ROI-to-voxel connectivity features in a standardized voxel-based feature space, which are subsequently processed using the BNT architecture to produce comparable subject-level embeddings. Experimental evaluations on sex classification and brain-connectome age prediction tasks demonstrate that our atlas-free BNT consistently outperforms state-of-the-art atlas-based methods, including elastic net, BrainGNN, Graphormer and the original BNT. Our atlas-free approach significantly improves the precision, robustness, and generalizability of brain network analyses. This advancement holds great potential to enhance neuroimaging biomarkers and clinical diagnostic tools for personalized precision medicine. Reproducible code is available at https://github.com/shuai-huang/atlas_free_bnt"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03306","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:43Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-27T03:15:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03255","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03255","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03255","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Wen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ziyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liwei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Liwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuenan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xuenan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jimin","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Jimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qitan","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Qitan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junlin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Junlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheqi","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Zheqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyuan","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Siyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SciTS: Scientific Time Series Understanding and Generation with LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-26T09:25:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:00:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T08:50:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:36:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The scientific reasoning ability of large language models (LLMs) has recently attracted significant attention. Time series, as a fundamental modality in scientific data, presents unique challenges that are often overlooked in current multimodal LLMs, which either encode numerical sequences as text or convert them into images. Such approaches may be insufficient for comprehensive scientific time series understanding and generation. Existing unified time series models typically specialise in either forecasting or analysis, and their effectiveness on non-periodic, heterogeneous scientific signals remains unclear. To address these gaps, we introduce SciTS, a benchmark spanning 12 scientific domains and 43 tasks, with over 50k+ instances, both univariate and multivariate signals ranging from $10^0$ to $10^7$ in length and up to 10~MHz in frequency. We benchmark 17 models, including text-only LLMs, multimodal LLMs, and unified time series models, and find that general-purpose LLMs exhibit stronger generalisability than specialised time series models, while representing time series as text or images limits their performance due to excessively long sequences and loss of numerical precision, respectively. We then introduce TimeOmni, a framework that equips LLMs with the ability to understand and generate time series while remaining compatible with general-purpose LLM training. This work fills a gap in both dedicated benchmarks and modelling frameworks for scientific time series, paving the way for LLMs to understand and generate complex temporal scientific data."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03255","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:27Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:26:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":372,"totalPages":4,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-06T16%3A00%3A00Z%20TO%202025-10-07T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D%20AND%20(%22language%20model%22%20OR%20%22language%20models%22%20OR%20LLM%20OR%20LLMs%20OR%20transformer%20OR%20transformers%20OR%20%22mixture%20of%20experts%22)\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1\u0026sort=registered","next":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=2\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-06T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-07T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+%22mixture+of+experts%22%29"}}